A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/83/8323a4292a4f06d66d456bfce1906cbc7e9933a3fac41dd5fb643e13d25f6a6e.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186054450 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186054450 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{Z}$\n2. $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n3. $\\mathbb{N}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186054450 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+$.\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+$.\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186054450 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186054451 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{ n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{ n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}?$\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$\n\n- $B\\nsubseteq A$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186054451 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}$?\n\n1. $A\\subseteq B$\n2. $B\\subseteq A$\n3. $B\\nsubseteq A$\n4. $B\\supseteq A$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186054451 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B$.\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10$, that is, $n\\geq 5$. So, we have\n$A= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}$.\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5$, that is, $n\\geq 3$. So, we have\n$B= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}$.\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B$.\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A$.\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B$, which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186054451 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10,$ that is, $n\\geq 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5,$ that is, $n\\geq 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A.$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186054456 1071 "Consider the set $A$, defined as\n\n$A = \\big \\{a_n \\,:\\,a_{n+1} = 2a_{n} - 1, \\, a_1 = 2,\\, n \\in \\mathbb N \\big\\}$.\n\nWrite down a set that's equivalent to $A$ using ellipsis notation." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186054456 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA = \\big \\{ a_n \\,:\\,a_{n+1} = 2a_{n} - 1, \\, a_1 = 2,\\, n \\in \\mathbb N \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWrite down a set that's equivalent to $A$ using ellipsis notation." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186054456 1074 "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence $a_n$, given by\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =2a_{n} - 1, \\quad a_1 =2,\\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2 \\\\\na_{2} &= 2a_{1} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(2) - 1 \\\\\n&= 3 \\\\\na_{3} &= 2a_{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(3) - 1 \\\\\n&= 5 \\\\\na_{4} &= 2a_{3} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(5) - 1 \\\\\n&= 9 \\\\\na_{5} &= 2a_{4} - 1 \\\\\n&= 2(9) - 1 \\\\\n&= 17 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{2,3,5,9,17,\\ldots \\right\\}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186054456 1074 "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence $a_n,$ given by\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =2a_{n} - 1, \\quad a_1 =2,\\qquad n \\in \\mathbb N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2{a}_{1}-1 \\\\  & =2(2)-1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2{a}_{2}-1 \\\\  & =2(3)-1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =2{a}_{3}-1 \\\\  & =2(5)-1 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =2{a}_{4}-1 \\\\  & =2(9)-1 \\\\  & =17 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{2,3,5,9,17,\\ldots \\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186054457 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\n- Subtract $x$ from $10.$\n\n- $y^2 -2  \\leq 3$\n\n- $1+z$ is a perfect square." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186054457 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\n1. Subtract $x$ from $10$.\n2. $y^2 -2 \\leq 3$\n3. $1+z$ is a perfect square." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186054457 1074 "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options.\n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it gives an instruction.\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n- Option III is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186054457 1074 "A *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false. Rather, it gives an instruction.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substi