$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8577 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8599 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8621 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8790 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8810 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8830 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8980 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8997 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9015 \\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2843 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2810 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2776 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3156 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3121 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8577 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8599 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8621 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8790 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8810 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8830 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8980 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8997 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9015 \\,$ |" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059434 1074 "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(-0.48 < Z < 1.18)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9011a3355b8261e153842a43be45e9bb07240d88421e37f7297a7d0f807e101.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$,\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z)\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.48 < Z < 1.18) &= P(Z < 1.18) - P(Z \\le - 0.48) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 1.18) - P(Z \\le - 0.48) \\\\\n&= Φ(1.18) - Φ(-0.48)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ(-0.48) &= 0.3156 \\\\\nΦ(1.18) &= 0.8810\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.48 < Z < 1.18) &= Φ(1.18) - Φ(-0.48) \\\\\n&= 0.8810 - 0.3156 \\\\\n&= 0.5654\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059434 1074 "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-0.48 <     Z <   1.18).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9011a3355b8261e153842a43be45e9bb07240d88421e37f7297a7d0f807e101.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.48<Z<1.18) & =P(Z<1.18)-P(Z\\le -0.48) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.18)-P(Z\\le -0.48) \\\\  & =Φ(1.18)-Φ(-0.48)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.48) & =0.3156 \\\\ Φ(1.18) & =0.8810\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.48<Z<1.18) & =Φ(1.18)-Φ(-0.48) \\\\  & =0.8810-0.3156 \\\\  & =0.5654.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059440 1071 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6$?\n\n1. $(a-2, b-6)$\n2. $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n3. $(a+2, b-6)$\n4. $(a-2,b+ 6)$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059440 1071 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6?$ \n\n- $(a-2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n\n- $(a+2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a-2,b+ 6)$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059440 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4$.\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4$. After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(a+2,b)$.\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6$. After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(a+2,b-6)$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n$b=a^4$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute\n\n$$\nx = a + 2\n$$\n\ninto this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (a + 2 - 2)^{4} - 6 \\\\\n&= a^{4} - 6 \\\\\n&= b - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when\n\n$$\nx = a + 2\n$$\n\n, we get $y = b - 6$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059440 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = (x-2)^4-6,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b-6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n\n$$\nb=a^4\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute $x = a + 2$ into this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+2-2{)}^{4}-6 \\\\  & ={a}^{4}-6 \\\\  & =b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $x = a + 2$, we get $y = b - 6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059441 1071 "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1$. What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059441 1071 "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1.$ What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059441 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1$.\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x)$. After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(3,0+1)=(3,1)$.\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x)$. After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(3,-(1)) = (3,-1)$.\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3$. After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(3,-1+3)=(3,2)$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that\n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f(3)=1\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -f(3) + 3 \\\\\n&=-1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059441 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,0+1)=(3,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1)) = (3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-1+3)=(3,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nf(3)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ Substituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+3 \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059442 1071 "![](images/d1/d12a03b0100b460301fa0a235ce73be995594091fdace654b5a3b9ada6aa259e.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059442 1071 "![](images/d1/d12a03b0100b460301fa0a235ce73be995594091fdace654b5a3b9ada6aa259e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x)$. What is the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059442 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(2x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$. So, we need to find the image of $(2,6)$, the local maximum of $y=f(x)$, under the transformation.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = f(2x)-1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,6)$.\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x)$. After this transformation, the point $(2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n$\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 2, 6 \\right) = (1,6)$.\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit downward and get the graph of $y = f(2x)-1$. After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(1,6-1) = (1,5)$.\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$ is $(1,5)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059442 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(2x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1.$ So, we need to 