\\cdot |\\,b\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot n \\\\  & =7\\cdot 3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\  & =7\\cdot 3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot n \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\sqrt{3}}{2}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059456 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $12$, the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ has magnitude $9$, the angle between the vectors is $105^\\circ$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$. Find $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{n}$. Give your answer to $2$ decimal places where appropriate." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059456 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $12,$ the vector $\\mathbf{b}$  has magnitude $9,$ the angle between the vectors is $105^\\circ$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{n}.$ Give your answer to $2$ decimal places where appropriate." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059456 1074 "We use the formula for the cross product and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\sin θ \\cdot n \\\\\n&= 12 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\sin 105^{∘} \\cdot n \\\\\n&\\approx 12 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 0.965926 \\cdot n \\\\\n&\\approx 104.32 \\cdot n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059456 1074 "We use the formula for the cross product and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot |\\,b\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot n \\\\  & =12\\cdot 9\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{105}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\  & ≈12\\cdot 9\\cdot 0.965926\\cdot n \\\\  & ≈104.32\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059457 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $4$, the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ has magnitude $6$, and $\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}$. Find $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059457 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $4,$ the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ has magnitude $6,$ and $\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059457 1074 "Suppose $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are nonzero vectors. Then,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nif and only if $\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059457 1074 "Suppose $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are nonzero vectors. Then, $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} \\parallel \\mathbf{b}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059458 1071 "Find the value of ![](images/8e/8e7935fce161e2a1ed706ec03bb5cd202e8c4650ba01ca5e4633f9f1c121d04a.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059458 1071 "Find the value of $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059458 1074 "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{vmatrix} &= 0\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{vmatrix}- (-3)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+ 6\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 0(8 + 96) + 3(8 + 48) + 6(-96 + 48) \\\\\n&= 0 + 3 \\cdot 56 + 6 \\cdot (-48) \\\\\n&= 168 - 288 \\\\\n&=-120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059458 1074 "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-3)|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}|+6|\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0(8+96)+3(8+48)+6(-96+48) \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot 56+6\\cdot (-48) \\\\  & =168-288 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059459 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- $A$ is a singular matrix\n\n![](images/21/21cd71e00c682ad1117b9ae126adcbeb8e4c55769c73eae1e1169534f2a01d4a.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059459 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\n1. $\\det(A) = 0$\n2. $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n3. $A$ is a singular matrix\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059459 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0$.\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}- 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+ 3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1(0 - 4) - 2(7 - 3) + 3(4 - 0) \\\\\n&=-4 - 2 \\cdot 4 + 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&=-4 - 8 + 12 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059459 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4)-2(7-3)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =-4-2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4-8+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059460 1071 "Given that the determinant of  $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ is $2,$ find the value of the constant $k.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186059460 1071 "Given that the determinant of $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is $2$, find the value of the constant $k$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059460 1074 "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} &= 3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}- 1\\begin{vmatrix}k & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+ 2\\begin{vmatrix}k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3(12 - 10) - 1(3k - 0) + 2(2k - 0) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2 - 1 \\cdot 3k + 2 \\cdot 2k \\\\\n&= 6 - 3k + 4k \\\\\n&= k + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $2$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk + 6 &= 2 \\\\\nk &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186059460 1074 "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(12-10)-1(3k-0)+2(2k-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3k+2\\cdot 2k \\\\  & =6-3k+4k \\\\  & =k+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $2,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+6 & =2 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063918 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graph II curves upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063918 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graph II curves upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063919 1071 "![](images/99/99a320e07046168bdd70d8168abcc6399de7e34044d6550a4d204a332110049b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* $f'(x) = 0$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063919 1071 "![](images/99/99a320e07046168bdd70d8168abcc6399de7e34044d6550a4d204a332110049b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* $f'(x) = 0?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063919 1074 "The derivative $f'(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](images/d9/d980345045691061434f86fb96138d0d4d20a38065e6606073974cd3b8f1d7bb.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($\\smile$) at $x=b$ only." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063919 1074 "The derivative $f'(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](images/d9/d980345045691061434f86fb96138d0d4d20a38065e6606073974cd3b8f1d7bb.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=d$, the graph is concave up ($\\smile$) at $x=b$ only." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063922 1071 "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1-x & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1^+} f(x)$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)= f(1)$\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063922 1071 "Consider the following function:\n${1 + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 1 \\\\ 1 - x\\end{vmatrix}x > 1$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1^+} f(x)$\n3. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)= f(1)$\n4. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063922 1074 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-x) \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ac7fdbbedfa59db5b49a3554cf1fb62b6d1fcafb346536f76599acdd82dd63.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063922 1074 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n2. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n3. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$?\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that\n$f(1)= 1+1= 2$.\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{-})(1 + x); = 1 + 1; = 2; lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{+})(1 - x); = 1 - 1; = 0$\nAs a result:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)$\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ac7fdbbedfa59db5b49a3554cf1fb62b6d1fcafb346536f76599acdd82dd63.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063923 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=2$?\n${3x + 2,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 2 \\\\ 6,\\end{vmatrix}x = 2; x^{3}, \\mid x > 2$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063923 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=2?$ \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+2, & x<2 \\\\ 6, & x=2 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063923 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$.\n\n- $f(2) = 6$, so $f(2)$ exists. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n$lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 2^{-})(3x + 2); = 3(2) + 2; = 8,; lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 2^{+})(x^{3}); = 2^{3}; = 8$.\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)=8.\\, \\, {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ $\\ \\[1ex]$\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)= 8$, while $f(2) = 6$. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x) \\neq f(2)\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063923 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2) = 6,$ so $f(2)$ exists. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(3x+2) \\\\  & =3(2)+2 \\\\  & =8, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)=8.\\, \\, {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ $\\ \\[1ex]$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)=  8,$ while $f(2) = 6.$ ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x) \\neq f(2).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063924 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ and $f(2)=2$, then what is the value of\n$\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}\\,$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063924 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ and $f(2)=2,$ then what is the value of \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}\\,?\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063924 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$. According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 2} f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=f(2)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}=\\sqrt{2^2-2\\cdot 2} = 0\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063924 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 2} f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=f(2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}=\\sqrt{2^2-2\\cdot 2} = 0.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063932 1071 "Calculate $F(\\pi)$ where $\\displaystyle F(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_1^x \\dfrac 1 t\\,\\textrm d t$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063932 1071 "Calculate $F(\\pi)$ where $\\displaystyle F(x) = \\int_1^x \\dfrac 1 t\\,\\textrm d t.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063932 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\pi$, we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\!\\left(\\pi\\right) = \\int_{1}^{\\pi} \\dfrac{1}{t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(π) &= \\int_{1}^{π}\\frac{1}{t}dt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\ln \\mid t \\mid \\right|_{1}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\ln π - \\ln 1 \\\\\n&= \\ln π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063932 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\pi,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\!\\left(\\pi\\right) = \\int_{1}^{\\pi} \\dfrac{1}{t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\pi ) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|\\,{|}_{1}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063933 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nf(t) = -t^{1/3}\n$$\n\nbe the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing, and let $F(T)$ be the amount of water in the pool after $T$ minutes. Note that $f(t)$ is negative to indicate that the volume is decreasing.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T) = F(0) + \\int_0^T f(t)\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(T) &= F(0) + \\int_{0}^{T}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= 100 - \\int_{0}^{T}t^{1/3}dt \\\\\n&= 100 - \\left.\\frac{3t^{4/3}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{T} \\\\\n&= 100 - (3(T)^{4/3})/(4) + (3(0)^{4/3})/(4) \\\\\n&= 100 - \\frac{3}{4}T^{4/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t$. We have\n\n$$\nF(t) = 100 - \\dfrac {3}{4}t^{4/3}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063933 1074 "Let $f(t) = -t^{1/3}$ be the rate at which the volume of water in the pool is changing, and let $F(T)$ be the amount of water in the pool after $T$ minutes. Note that $f(t)$ is negative to indicate that the volume is decreasing.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nF(T) \t\t\t= F(0) + \\int_0^T f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(T) & =F(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-{\\int }_{0}^{T}{t}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3{t}^{4/3}}{4}{|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3(T)^{4/3}}{4}+\\frac{3(0)^{4/3}}{4} \\\\  & =100-\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{4/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nF(t) = 100 - \\dfrac {3}{4}t^{4/3}.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063934 1071 "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Given $\\displaystyle g(t)= \\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{t} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x$, determine the value of $g(3)$.\n\n![](images/7e/7eb2c5d91fde9279e2533ff77d86ea30e9c21c3a026d26e84e4d04293da6f63a.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063934 1071 "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Given $\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{-2}^{t} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$\n\n![](images/7e/7eb2c5d91fde9279e2533ff77d86ea30e9c21c3a026d26e84e4d04293da6f63a.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063934 1074 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3$, we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bf473cbddef213fb959112fb72ca9a13d9aae4d72c64ce5a68110f349e34d33.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x \\in [-2,0]$, for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_1$.\n- $x \\in [0,3]$, for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_2$.\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x \\in [-2,0]$, we compute the area $A_1$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot (2 + 3); = 5$\n- To find the second integral where $x \\in [0,3]$, we compute the area $A_2$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot (2 + 4); = 9$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(3) &= \\int_{-2}^{3}f(x)dx = A_{1} + A_{2} = 5 + 9 = 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063934 1074 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bf473cbddef213fb959112fb72ca9a13d9aae4d72c64ce5a68110f349e34d33.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x \\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_1.$\n\n- $x \\in [0,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area $A_2.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3) = \\int_{-2}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{3} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x \\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area $A_1$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x \\in [0,3],$ we compute the area $A_2$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (2+4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+9=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063957 1071 "Find the value of $f(x) = \\dfrac{e^{\\left\\lfloor x+2 \\right\\rfloor}}{2x+1}$ at $x = 0.75$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063957 1071 "Find the value of $f(x) = \\dfrac{e^{\\left\\lfloor x+2 \\right\\rfloor}}{2x+1}$ at $x = 0.75.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063957 1074 "Suppose $x$ is a real number. The greatest integer (or floor) function $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ equals the greatest integer that is less than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lfloor x \\rfloor = n,\n\\quad\\text{where}\\quad\nn \\leq x \\lt n+1,\\quad  n\\in \\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x = 0.75,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.75) & =\\frac{{e}^{⌊0.75+2⌋}}{2(0.75)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{⌊2.75⌋}}{2.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the greatest integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\lfloor 2.75\\right\\rfloor = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.75) & =\\frac{{e}^{⌊2.75⌋}}{2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2.5} \\\\  & =2.955\\,62… \\\\  & ≈2.96,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063957 1074 "Suppose $x$ is a real number. The greatest integer (or floor) function $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ equals the greatest integer that is less than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lfloor x \\rfloor = n, \\quad\\text{where}\\quad n \\leq x \\lt n+1,\\quad n\\in \\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x = 0.75$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0.75) &= (e^{⌊0.75 + 2⌋})/(2(0.75) + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{⌊2.75⌋}}{2.5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the greatest integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\lfloor 2.75\\right\\rfloor = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0.75) &= \\frac{e^{⌊2.75⌋}}{2.5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{2}}{2.5} \\\\\n&= 2.95562… \\\\\n&\\approx 2.96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063958 1071 "Find the value of $f(x) = \\left \\lceil e^{6x-5} \\right \\rceil$ at $x = 1.25$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063958 1071 "Find the value of $f(x) = \\left \\lceil e^{6x-5} \\right \\rceil$ at $x = 1.25.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063958 1074 "Suppose that $x$ is a real number. The least integer (or ceiling) function $\\lceil x \\rceil$ equals the smallest integer that is greater than or equal to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lceil x \\rceil = n, \\quad\\text{where}\\quad n-1 \\lt x \\leq n, \\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x = 1.25$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1.25) &= ⌈e^{6(1.25) - 5}⌉ \\\\\n&= ⌈e^{7.5 - 5}⌉ \\\\\n&= ⌈e^{2.5}⌉\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the least integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\left \\lceil e^{2.5} \\right \\rceil = \\left \\lceil 12.182\\,49 \\ldots \\right \\rceil = 13\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1.25) &= ⌈e^{2.5}⌉ \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063958 1074 "Suppose that $x$ is a real number. The least integer (or ceiling) function $\\lceil x \\rceil$ equals the smallest integer that is greater than or equal to $x{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\lceil x \\rceil = n,\n\\quad\\text{where}\\quad\nn-1 \\lt x \\leq n, \\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x = 1.25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.25) & =⌈{e}^{6(1.25)-5}⌉ \\\\  & =⌈{e}^{7.5-5}⌉ \\\\  & =⌈{e}^{2.5}⌉.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the least integer function, we have\n\n$$\n\\left \\lceil e^{2.5} \\right \\rceil = \\left \\lceil 12.182\\,49 \\ldots \\right \\rceil = 13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.25) & =⌈{e}^{2.5}⌉ \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063986 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^2 + \\dfrac {1}{x}$ at the point where $x=2$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063986 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^2 + \\dfrac {1}{x}$ at the point where $x=2.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063986 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2+\\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to $f'(2)$. Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + x^{-1}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot x - x^{-2} \\\\\n&= 2x - x^{-2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= 2(2) - (2)^{-2} \\\\\n&= 4 - \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063986 1074 "Let $f(x)=x^2+\\dfrac 1 x.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to $f'(2).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot x-{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =2x-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =2(2)-(2{)}^{-2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $\\dfrac{15}{4}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063989 1071 "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^4 + 3\\sqrt [3]{x^2}$ at the point $(1,4)$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063989 1071 "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^4 + 3\\sqrt [3]{x^2}$ at the point $(1,4)?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186063989 1074 "This time, we'll use\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nnotation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} + 3\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} + 3x^{2/3}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot x^{3} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot x^{-1/3} \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + 2x^{-1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x = 1} &= 4(1)^{3} + 2(1)^{-1/3} \\\\\n&= 4 + 2 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $6$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186063989 1074 "This time, we'll use $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {x}^{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{-1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =4(1{)}^{3}+2(1{)}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $6.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186064004 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x)$. Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P$, determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186064004 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186064004 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{4 + 5x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= - (22)/((4 + 5x)^{2}) \\\\\nf^{′}(-1) &= -22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x)$, we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(-5) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(-5))) \\\\\n&= (1)/(f^{′}(-1)) \\\\\n&= (1)/((-22)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{22}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186064004 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186064005 1071 "![](images/8f/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x)$. The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x)$.\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x)$. If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$, what is $(f^{-1})'(0)$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186064005 1071 "![](images/8f/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186064005 1074 "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1}$, we have\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(0) = 2\n$$\n\nWe're also told that\n\n$$\nf'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(x) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(x)))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(0) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(0))) \\\\\n&= (1)/(f^{′}(2)) \\\\\n&= (1)/((-\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186064005 1074 "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186064006 1071 "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x)$, and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186064006 1071 "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186064006 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1)\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(1)=2\n$$\n\n$g(1)=-3$, $g'(1)=1$, and $f'(2)=-1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1} \\cdot g)^{′}(1) &= (g(1))/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(1))) + g^{′}(1)f^{-1}(1) \\\\\n&= (-3)/(f^{′}(2)) + 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{-3}{-1} + 2 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186064006 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186064007 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1$. Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186064007 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186064007 1074 "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2)$, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2)$. To do this, we solve the equation\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(2) = x\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(2) &= x \\\\\nf(x) &= 2 \\\\\n3e^{x} - 1 &= 2 \\\\\n3e^{x} &= 3 \\\\\ne^{x} &= 1 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(2) = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3e^{x} - 1 \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 3e^{x} \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= 3e^{0} \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f^{-1})^{′}(2) &= (1)/(f^{′}(f^{-1}(2))) \\\\\n&= (1)/(f^{′}(0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186064007 1074 "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068435 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 2x + 3$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068435 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 2x + 3.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068435 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{1}}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{blue}{1}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2 + 2\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068435 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{1}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{blue}{1}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2  + 2.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068436 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 12x + 40$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068436 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 12x + 40.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068436 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{6}}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{40}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{blue}{6}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2 +4\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068436 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{6}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{40}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{blue}{6}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  - {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2  +4.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068437 1071 "Solve the equation $x^2 -10x = -24$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^2 -10x +25$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068437 1071 "Solve the equation $x^2 -10x = -24.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^2 -10x +25$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068437 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\nx^2 -10x +25 = (x-5)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 10x &= -24 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot x &= -24 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot x + 5^{2} - 5^{2} &= -24 \\\\\n(x - 5)^{2} - 25 &= -24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $25$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 5)^{2} - 25 + 25 &= -24 + 25 \\\\\n(x - 5)^{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 5)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x - 5)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n\\mid x - 5 \\mid &= 1 \\\\\nx - 5 &= ± 1 \\\\\nx &= 5 ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= 5 - 1 = 4 \\\\\nx_{2} &= 5 + 1 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068437 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\nx^2 -10x +25 = (x-5)^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x & =-24 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x & =-24 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =-24 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}-25 & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $25$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2}-25+25 & =-24+25 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ |x-5| & =1 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-1=4 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =5+1=6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068453 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068453 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068453 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= \\frac{x^{2}}{y} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{x^{2}}{y} \\\\\ny\\frac{dy}{dx} &= x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\inty\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\intx^{2}dx \\\\\n\\inty\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\intx^{2}dx \\\\\n\\intydy &= \\intx^{2}dx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}y^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}y^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + C \\\\\ny^{2} &= \\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + 2C \\\\\ny &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + 2C} \\\\\ny &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + K}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068453 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C.\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068454 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068454 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068454 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} + 2xy &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2xy \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int(-2x)dx \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{y}dy &= -2\\intxdx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid y \\mid &= -x^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x)$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln \\mid y \\mid &= -x^{2} + C \\\\\n\\mid y \\mid &= e^{-x^{2} + C} \\\\\n\\mid y \\mid &= e^{-x^{2}} \\cdot e^{C} \\\\\n\\mid y \\mid &= Ke^{-x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$ and $K > 0$.\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068454 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x