 x > -5$, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers:\n\n$$\n-5 < x < 2\n$$\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.\n\n![](images/f5/f514a2aca3b3995ea2b1dc970a3b8f16a476ec0f9bb0178909d86ff670503354.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068480 1074 "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual. \n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & <1-2x<11 \\\\ -3-1 & <1-2x-1<11-1 \\\\ -4 & <-2x<10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $-2.$ Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & <-2x<10 \\\\ \\frac{-4}{-2} & >\\frac{-2x}{-2}>\\frac{10}{-2} \\\\ 2 & >x>-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $2 > x > -5$, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers: \n\n$$\n-5 < x < 2\n$$\n\nWe can represent this solution using a number line, as follows.\n\n![](images/f5/f514a2aca3b3995ea2b1dc970a3b8f16a476ec0f9bb0178909d86ff670503354.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068504 1071 "Write the equation $x^2 + 6x +5= 0$ in the form $(x+p)^2=q$, and hence solve the equation." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068504 1071 "Write the equation $x^2 + 6x +5= 0$ in the form $(x+p)^2=q,$ and hence solve the equation." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068504 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 6x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 6x &= -5 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x &= -5 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} &= -5 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2}) - 9 &= -5 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} - 9 &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)^{2} - 9 &= -5 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} &= -5 + 9 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=3$ and $q=4$. Now, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 3)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n\\mid x + 3 \\mid &= 2 \\\\\nx + 3 &= ± 2 \\\\\nx &= -3 ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= -3 + 2 =-1 \\\\\nx_{2} &= -3 - 2 =-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068504 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x & =-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot x & =-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2} & =-5 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2})-9 & =-5 \\\\ (x+3{)}^{2}-9 & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3{)}^{2}-9 & =-5 \\\\ (x+3{)}^{2} & =-5+9 \\\\ (x+3{)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, $p=3$ and $q=4.$ Now, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3{)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x+3| & =2 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-3+2=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-3-2=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068505 1071 "Solve the equation $x^2-2x-1=0$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068505 1071 "Solve the equation $x^2-2x-1=0.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068505 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 1 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot x &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot x + 1^{2} - 1^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 2x + 1) - 1 &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} - 1 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square and solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 1)^{2} - 1 &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= 2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x - 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\mid x - 1 \\mid &= \\sqrt{2} \\\\\nx - 1 &= ± \\sqrt{2} \\\\\nx &= 1 ± \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= 1 + \\sqrt{2}, \\\\\nx_{2} &= 1 - \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068505 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =1 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)-1 & =1 \\\\ (x-1{)}^{2}-1 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square and solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1{)}^{2}-1 & =1 \\\\ (x-1{)}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1+\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068510 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2-2x + 3}\\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068510 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2-2x + 3}\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068510 1074 "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2-2x + 3 & = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ (1^2 - 1^2) + 3 \\\\[3pt] & = (x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ 1^2) - 1 + 3 \\\\[3pt] & = (x-1)^2+2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{x^{2} - 2x + 3}dx &= \\int(1)/((x - 1)^{2} + 2)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int(1)/(1 + ((x - 1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arctan u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{x^{2} - 2x + 3}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int(1)/(1 + ((x - 1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\arctan u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{2}}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068510 1074 "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nx^2-2x + 3\n& = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ (1^2 - 1^2) + 3 \\[3pt]\n& = (x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ 1^2) - 1 + 3 \\[3pt]\n& = (x-1)^2+2\n}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n=\n\\arctan u + C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068511 1071 "Calculate ${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186068511 1071 "Calculate ${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186068511 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 4x^{2} &= - (4x^{2} - 4x) \\\\\n&= - ((2x)^{2} - 2 \\cdot 2x + 1 - 1) \\\\\n&= - ((2x - 1)^{2} - 1) \\\\\n&= 1 - (2x - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^{2}}}dx &= \\int(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - (2x - 1)^{2}})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(2x - 1)$.\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$u =2x - 1$.\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac12\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^{2}}}dx &= \\int(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - (2x - 1)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}du \\\\