ince statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105460 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n![](images/2b/2bad707ee54e56c4171b99b13a36135522ab83069ab3b0291f3254850502226d.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105460 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n${2x^{3} + kx^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105460 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}(2x^{3} + kx^{2})dx &= [\\frac{x^{4}}{2} + \\frac{kx^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{k}{3}] - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{3} + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105460 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{k{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{k}{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{align