}{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105465 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz$, where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$.\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below.\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105465 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n![](images/e4/e44b57bad88dcc1c13680f0e8f110696c6975b16c1d9553138fb6aeb4cadb8f9.png)\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105465 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)f(x, y, g(x, y))\\sqrt{1 + (2x)^{2} + (-1)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x(x^{2} - y)\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}(x^{3} - xy)\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2}}dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105465 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,x({x}^{2}-y)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({x}^{3}-xy)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105466 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S'$, the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$?\n\n1. $(-2,0)$\n2. $(0,0)$\n3. $(2,0)$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105466 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n![](images/3f/3ff8dc37a532cfe49a2fbd8c02a1b27c50642d8e4c54def6984f3596f68065fa.png)\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n- $(-2,0)$\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(2,0)$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105466 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0)$, $(0,0)$, and $(2,0)$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105466 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0),$ $(0,0),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105467 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T$.\n\n![](images/85/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105467 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n![](images/54/5414050f8a5a5208b4424333e947919ce196687f8bfb0e60c2ced0a04a2695cc.png)  Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](images/85/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105467 1074 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n![](images/95/95c80fe2654c8f6eb4b65492eab1efb07f568375b2960b1633e5e2e1a7741a8b.png)\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2),$ and $(-2,4).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/3f/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105467 1074 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2)$, and $(-2,4)$. Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/3f/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105468 1071 "![](images/89/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105468 1071 "![](images/89/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n![](images/50/501ab476f0e420892fee6c87307dd33f260affc1533019621b366b5462920322.png)\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105468 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0$, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0$, we have two additional subcases:If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0)$, the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105468 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105469 1071 "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-1,7)$ and parallel to ![](images/48/48f9afd751f6424fe465e494fd254593de065563231a1f5df07741789969c1af.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105469 1071 "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-1,7)$ and parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105469 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105469 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is ![](images/92/9264ecd5e62e51eec08ae686461f8b3d99aadbddd2363b5c700c72839d59524f.png)\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105470 1071 "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105470 1071 "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105470 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= PQ^(→) \\\\\n&= ⟨3, 2, 7⟩ - ⟨1,-1, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 3, 5⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105470 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,7⟩-⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105471 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,2,0)$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}= \\langle -4,1,1 \\rangle$. Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $PA=6\\sqrt{2}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105471 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,2,0)$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}= \\langle -4,1,1 \\rangle.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $PA=6\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105471 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= p + tv \\\\\nr &= ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ + t⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA^(→) &= OA^(→) - OP^(→) \\\\\n&= (⟨1, 2, 0⟩ + t⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩) - ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= t⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 4t, t, t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nPA=6\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\mid PA^(→) \\mid &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\mid ⟨ - 4t, t, t⟩ \\mid &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-4t)^{2} + t^{2} + t^{2}} &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{18t^{2}} &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n± 3t\\sqrt{2} &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\nt &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\nOA_{1}^(→) = ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ + 2⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩ = ⟨ - 7, 4, 2⟩,; OA_{2}^(→) = ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ - 2⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩ = ⟨9, 0,-2⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105471 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩)-⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=6\\sqrt{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PA & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{18{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\pm 3t\\sqrt{2} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨1,2,0⟩+2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨-7,4,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨1,2,0⟩-2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨9,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105472 1071 "Using $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j},\\mathbf{k}$ notation, find a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$, where $P(-4,1,2)$ and $Q(-2,5,-1)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105472 1071 "Using $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j},\\mathbf{k}$ notation, find a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(-4,1,2)$ and $Q(-2,5,-1).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105472 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= PQ^(→) \\\\\n&= OQ^(→) - OP^(→) \\\\\n&= (-2i + 5j - k) - (-4i + j + 2k) \\\\\n&= 2i + 4j - 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{r} = (-4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}) + t (2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\, \\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= (-4i + j + 2k) + t(2i + 4j - 3k) \\\\\n&= (2t - 4)i + (4t + 1)j + (2 - 3t)k, t ∈ [0, 1]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105472 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+5\\,j-k)-(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{r} = (-4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}) + t (2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\, \\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(4t+1)\\,j+(2-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105484 1071 "Suppose that $\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=8, \\mid \\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|=4$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$. Find the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ in terms of the vector $\\math