 (1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-4) + 5 \\cdot 0)/(5^{2} + (-4)^{2} + 0^{2})b \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{41}⟨5,-4, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{85}{41},-\\frac{68}{41}, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105485 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+(-3)\\cdot (-4)+5\\cdot 0}{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{41}⟨5,-4,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{85}{41},-\\frac{68}{41},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105486 1071 "Let ![](images/e1/e199e48633dbaeeb817da846163a9c8a9bf1ae3355c69b56461b25a13ef73406.png) and ![](images/4c/4ce6e454ff96976ddeb735d1ab42562fe3b7eb16f16e7c0402d8040f9586907b.png). Find the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186105486 1071 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105486 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y} + a_{z}b_{z}}{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2} + b_{z}^{2}}b \\\\\n&= (1 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\cdot (-1))/(1^{2} + 5^{2} + (-1)^{2})b \\\\\n&= (0)/(1^{2} + 5^{2} + (-1)^{2})b \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186105486 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 5+1\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}b \\\\  & =⟨0,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110293 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tan x\\cdot \\cot x = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the above identities, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\tan x \\cdot (\\cot x + \\tan x) &= 2\\tan x \\cdot \\cot x + 2\\tan^{2} x \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 1 + 2\\tan^{2} x \\\\\n&= 2(1 + \\tan^{2} x) \\\\\n&= 2\\sec^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\tan x\\cdot(\\cot x+\\tan{x})= 2\\sec^2x\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186110293 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}\n$$\n\nAlso, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tan x\\cdot \\cot x = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the above identities, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2{tan}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\text{ }\\,(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\,{sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\tan x\\cdot(\\cot x+\\tan{x})= 2\\sec^2x.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110294 1071 "Express $(1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x )$ in terms of $\\tan x$ only." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110294 1071 "Express $(1-\\sec x)(1+\\sec x)$ in terms of $\\tan x$ only." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186110294 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x ) = 1 - \\sec^2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{sec}^{2}⁡x & =1-(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =1-1-{tan}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(1-\\sec x )(1+\\sec x )= -\\tan^2 x.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110294 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we expand the parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(1-\\sec x)(1+\\sec x) = 1 - \\sec^2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - \\sec^{2} x &= 1 - (1 + \\tan^{2} x) \\\\\n&= 1 - 1 - \\tan^{2} x \\\\\n&=-\\tan^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(1-\\sec x)(1+\\sec x)= -\\tan^2 x\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186110296 1074 "Consider the line $y = 2x - 1$. Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny \\leq 2x - 1\n$$\n\nNow consider the line\n\n$$\ny = -x+2\n$$\n\nSince the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny \\geq -x + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines our shaded region is\n\n$$\n{y \\le 2x - 1; y \\ge - x + 2\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186110296 1074 "Consider the line $y = 2x - 1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny \\leq 2x - 1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y = -x+2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny \\geq -x + 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines our shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 2x-1 \\\\ y\\ge -x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110340 1071 "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y= -\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3$?\n\n1. $y = 5x + 1$\n2. $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$\n3. $y = -\\dfrac{x}{2} + 5$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186110340 1071 "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y= -\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3?$\n\n- $y = 5x + 1$\n\n- $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$\n\n- $y = -\\dfrac{x}{2} + 5$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110340 1074 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1$.\n\nThe line\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3\n$$\n\nis in slope-intercept form, and its slope is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3\n$$\n\nhas a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{5}{2} = -1\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1$, we conclude that the line\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3\n$$\n\nis perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186110340 1074 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-\\dfrac{2}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$ has a slope of $\\dfrac{5}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left( -\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{5}{2} = -1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$ is perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110341 1071 "The lines $y=2x+9$ and $3y+kx = 12$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186110341 1071 "The lines $y=2x+9$ and $3y+kx = 12$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110341 1074 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1$.\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+9$ is $2$.\n- To find the slope of $3y+kx = 12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n$3y + kx|= 12; 3y|=-kx + 12; y|=-\\frac{k}{3}x + 4$\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3y +kx = 12$ is $-\\dfrac{k}{3}$.\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot\\left(-\\dfrac{k}{3}\\right) = -1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186110341 1074 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+9$ is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of  $3y+kx = 12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+kx & =12 \\\\ 3y & =-kx+12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{k}{3}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3y +kx = 12$ is $-\\dfrac{k}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot\\left(- \\dfrac{k}{3}\\right) = -1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110342 1071 "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(3,4)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186110342 1071 "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(3,4).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186110342 1074 "Since the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take 