 functions are differentiable at the point $x=-1$?\n\n- $f(x) = x^5+1$\n- $g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186114927 1071 "Which of the following functions are differentiable at the point $x=-1?$\n\n- $f(x) = x^5+1$\n\n- $g(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186114927 1074 "Let's analyze each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial and is therefore differentiable and continuous for all $x$.\n- The function $g(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=-1$, so it is not differentiable at $x=-1$ either.\n\nIn conclusion, only $f(x)$ is differentiable at the point $x=-1$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186114927 1074 "Let's analyze each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial and is therefore differentiable and continuous for all $x.$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=-1,$ so it is not differentiable at $x=-1$ either.\n\nIn conclusion, only $f(x)$ is differentiable at the point $x=-1.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186114928 1074 "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a vertical tangent, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has a removable discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0, 1, 2.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186114928 1074 "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a vertical tangent, so it's not differentiable there.\n- At $x=1$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n- At $x=2$ the function has a removable discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0, 1, 2$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119503 1071 "The set $\\{ \\mathbf{a_1},\\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n![](images/2f/2fc772925dd6b793bdcb3a1eca96ea7e7f5fed17425f3500638340e047170c60.png)\n\n![](images/f7/f73258610652de9a656f3c4ed202241c58c98f18391b1393dabe62d92d9bef7d.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119503 1071 "The set $\\{\\mathbf{a_1},\\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119503 1074 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = 0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nTo see why, notice that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}a_{1} - a_{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 +x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 1 3\n$$\n\nand $x_2=-1$, which is different from $x_1=x_2=0$. Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119503 1074 "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n![](images/21/210a5c50467861cdfe48b46f2098212bbf387270b420a05394e318a0e0b48c74.png)\nTo see why, notice that \n![](images/91/91a29b57d32ed8c8e702f2988f7714ffc343ed6b4707b86b49b708ffad5c8bc8.png)\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1  +x_2  \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=\\dfrac 1 3$ and $x_2=-1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119504 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](images/e9/e9d8c1ab6f31d1ca580343f16d10eb55b6aae24db679129540a30b552cedaf19.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0.$\n\n- The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119504 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$.\n2. $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0$.\n3. The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119504 1074 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n$a_{1} - a_{2} - 2a_{3}|= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}4 + 0 - 4 \\\\ -1 - 1 + 2 \\\\ 0 + 0 - 14\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}; \\ne 0$\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n${4x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 0; - x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} = 0; 7x_{3} = 0$.\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n${-x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} = 0; 4x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 0; 7x_{3} = 0$\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119504 1074 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119505 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](images/8d/8d88cae0e40e7a377314e359086dc45fa0460c17ed55fe379cc15537886b2623.png)\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2,$ and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119505 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2$, and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n2. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n3. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119505 1074 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$, and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{2x_{1} - x_{2} + 2x_{3} = 0; 4x_{1} + 4x_{2} + 5x_{3} = 0; 6x_{2} + x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2$, while $x_3$ is the free variable.\n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119505 1074 "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ while $x_3$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119506 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=5+3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n2. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n3. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n4. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119506 1071 "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=5+3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119506 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces:\n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=5+3x{:}$\n$y|= 5 + 3x; (0)|= 5 + 3(0); 0|= 5\\cdot$\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$. Then,\n$a + b|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 + (-1) \\\\ 5 + 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$.\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation $y=5+3x$ gives the following:\n$y|= 5 + 3x; 7|= 5 + 3(-1); 7|= 2\\cdot$\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L$, this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119506 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does 