d $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$ at the point $\\left(3, 1\\right)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119517 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(y + 2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}(y + 2y^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}(y) + 2\\frac{d}{dy}(y^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 2 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}y^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 3y^{1/2} \\\\\n&= 1 + 3\\sqrt{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{1 + 3\\sqrt y}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,1)$, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(x, y) = (3, 1)} &= \\frac{1}{1 + 3\\sqrt{1}} = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119517 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2{y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =1+3{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =1+3\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} =  \\dfrac{1}{1 + 3\\sqrt y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,1)} & =\\frac{1}{1+3\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119518 1071 "What is the equation of the tangent to the curve $x = 2y^2 -y + 1$ at the point $(2,1)$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119518 1071 "What is the equation of the tangent to the curve $x = 2y^2 -y + 1$ at the point $(2,1)?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119518 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(2y^{2} - y + 1) \\\\\n&= 2\\frac{d}{dy}(y^{2}) - \\frac{d}{dy}(y) + \\frac{d}{dy}(1) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (2y) - 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 4y - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac1{4y - 1}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(x, y) = (2, 1)} \\\\\n&= (1)/(4(1) - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 2) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{2}{3} + 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119518 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2y)-1+0 \\\\  & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac1{4y - 1}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(2,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119519 1071 "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119519 1071 "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119519 1074 "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t$. At this point, the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P$. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= i + j + k \\\\\nti + t^{2}j + t^{3}k &= i + j + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j$, and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n{t = 1; t^{2} = 1; t^{3} = 1\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1$. This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1)$.\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3})k \\\\\n&= i + 2tj + 3t^{2}k \\\\\nr^{′}(1) &= i + 2(1)j + 3(1)^{2}k \\\\\n&= i + 2j + 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k$.\n\n![](images/ba/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119519 1074 "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](images/ba/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119520 1071 "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119520 1071 "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119520 1074 "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119520 1074 "Let $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nAt this point, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}), \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}), 4(\\frac{π}{2})^{2}⟩ = ⟨0, 1, π^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(4t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\sin t, \\cos t, 8t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) &= ⟨ - \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}), \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}), 8(\\frac{π}{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 0, 4π⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nR(u) = ⟨0, 1, π^{2}⟩ + u⟨ - 1, 0, 4π⟩, u ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119521 1071 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119521 1071 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119521 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} + t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t)j \\\\\n&= (2t + 1)i + (2t - 1)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n(2t + 1)i + (2t - 1)j = c(3i + j)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{2t + 1 = 3c; 2t - 1 = c\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1$. Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t + 1 &= 3c \\\\\n2t + 1 &= 3(2t - 1) \\\\\n2t + 1 &= 6t - 3 \\\\\n6t - 2t &= 1 + 3 \\\\\n4t &= 4 \\\\\nt &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(1) &= (1^{2} + 1)i + (1^{2} - 1)j = 2i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0)$.\n\n![](images/0f/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119521 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](images/0f/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119522 1071 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle$, find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119522 1071 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119522 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} + 1), \\frac{d}{dt}(6t + 3)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2t, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨3, 1⟩ \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨3, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨2t, 6⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot 2t + 1 \\cdot 6 &= 0 \\\\\n6t + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(-1) &= ⟨(-1)^{2} + 1, 6(-1) + 3⟩ = ⟨2,-3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119522 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 6t+6 & =0 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}+1\\,,\\,6(-1)+3⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119523 1071 "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, -2t, t^2 \\rangle$ at the point where $t = 1$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119523 1071 "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, -2t, t^2 \\rangle$ at the point where $t = 1.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119523 1074 "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t)$, we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\\\right|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$, $\\mathbf r'(1)$, and $\\|\\mathbf r'(1)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t), \\frac{d}{dt}(-2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-2, 2t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(1) &= ⟨1,-2, 2⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(1)‖ &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(1), \\frac{d}{dt}(-2), 2\\frac{d}{dt}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0, 2⟩ \\\\\nr^{″}(1) &= ⟨0, 0, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(1) \\cdot r^{″}(1) &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n&=-4i - 2j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(1) \\cdot r^{″}(1)‖ &= \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{20} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(1) &= (‖r^{′}(1) \\cdot r^{″}(1)‖)/(‖r^{′}(1)‖^{3}) = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3^{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119523 1074 "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(1),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(1)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119524 1071 "Using the cross product formula for curvature, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2t, 1+t^2\\rangle$ as a function of $t$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119524 1071 "Using the cross product formula for curvature, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2t, 1+t^2\\rangle$ as a function of $t.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119524 1074 "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t)$, we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\\\right|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(1 + t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 2t⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{2^{2} + (2t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 4t^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t)⟩ = ⟨0, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t) &= ⟨2, 2t, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{vmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}2k \\\\\n&= 4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\| = 4\n$$\n\nand therefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(t) &= (‖r^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t)‖)/(‖r^{′}(t)‖^{3}) = (4)/((2\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}})^{3}) = (1)/(2(1 + t^{2})^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119524 1074 "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩=⟨0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2,\\,2t,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\| = 4,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{(2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119525 1071 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2, \\: 2t, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186119525 1071 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2, \\: 2t, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119525 1074 "The curvature $\\kappa (t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative $\\mathbf{r}''(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩=⟨2,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,2,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{8({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}=\\frac{1}{2({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186119525 1074 "The curvature $\\kappa (t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\\\right|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2t, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(2t)^{2} + (2)^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4t^{2} + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(t^{2} + 1)} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative $\\mathbf{r}''(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2), \\frac{d}{dt}(0)⟩ = ⟨2, 0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t) &= ⟨2t, 2, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 0, 0⟩ \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n&=-