$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\left(\\dfrac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(x) = y^2 \\ln{x}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\nf(x+h,y) = y^2\\ln(x+h)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\left(\\dfrac{y^2\\ln(x+h) -y^2\\ln{x}}{h}\\right)\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124207 1074 "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\left(\\dfrac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x) = y^2 \\ln{x},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y) = y^2\\ln(x+h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\left( \\dfrac{y^2\\ln(x+h) -y^2\\ln{x} }{h}\\right).\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124208 1071 "For the function $f(x,y) = x^2y^2+\\sin{x}\\cos{y}$, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124208 1071 "For the function $f(x,y) = x^2y^2+\\sin{x}\\cos{y},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124208 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x$, treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y^{2} + \\sin x\\cos y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sin x\\cos y) \\\\\n&= y^{2} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) + \\cos y \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= y^{2} \\cdot (2x) + \\cos y \\cdot (\\cos x) \\\\\n&= 2xy^{2} + \\cos x\\cos y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right|_{(π, 2π)} &= 2 \\cdot π \\cdot (2π)^{2} + \\cos π \\cdot \\cos 2π \\\\\n&= 2π \\cdot 4π^{2} + (-1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 8π^{3} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124208 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,2\\pi )} & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (2\\pi )^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4{\\pi }^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8{\\pi }^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124214 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 +4y^2-4=0$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124214 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 +4y^2-4=0.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124214 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y)= x^2 +4y^2 -4=0\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= λ∇g(x, y) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y), g_{y}(x, y)⟩ \\\\\n⟨2x, 2y⟩ &= λ⟨2x, 8y⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{2x = 2λx; 2y = 8λy⇒{x(1 - λ) = 0; y(4λ - 1) = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4 =0$ implies\n$4y^{2} - 4|= 0⇒y = ± 1$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,1 \\right), \\left(0,-1 \\right)$.\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n$y(4(1) - 1) = 0⇒y = 0$.\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4 =0$ implies\n$x^{2} - 4|= 0⇒x = ± 2$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(-2,0 \\right), \\left(2,0 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n$(0, \\pm 1), \\qquad (\\pm 2,0)$.\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 2, 0)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 1)$, and its minimum value is $1$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124214 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 +4y^2 -4=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4\\lambda -1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,1 \\right), \\left(0,-1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4(1)-1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(-2,0 \\right), \\left(2,0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0, \\pm 1), \\qquad (\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 2, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 1),$ and its minimum value is $1.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124215 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = 2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y) = (0,-2),$ so the global maximum point is $(0,-2,2).$\n\n![](images/f8/f80d19126252f6ab7097cf528aec9726c2708f48c815c026f632fccd77cf74d4.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124215 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum is $2$. The function reaches this value when\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (0,-2)\n$$\n\nso the global maximum point is $(0,-2,2)$.\n\n![](images/f8/f80d19126252f6ab7097cf528aec9726c2708f48c815c026f632fccd77cf74d4.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124216 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)\n$$\n\nfor all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b)$.\n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A, B$, and $D$. Hence, $A, B$, and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D(x_D,y_D,z_D)$, there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that\n\n$$\nf(x_D,y_D) = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nHowever, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict,\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)\n$$\n\n, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/cd/cdb83ac891799788c8289f7e44902bf6be008cb13a7bf27f330b48113df021d1.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124216 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A, B,$ and $D.$ Hence, $A, B,$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D(x_D,y_D,z_D),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f(x_D,y_D) = f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/cd/cdb83ac891799788c8289f7e44902bf6be008cb13a7bf27f330b48113df021d1.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124217 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n![](images/2a/2ae5b42ae953d1b9d3fd642e9938924a9b2bc439dee1ea52c868f186798b1520.png)\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3,$ what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124217 1071 "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3$, what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124217 1074 "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if $\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ In such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ about $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})\n+\n\\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T\n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ Hence, $\\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124217 1074 "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ about\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\ngiven by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) (\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f$.\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{1} &= -1 < 0 \\\\\nλ_{2} &= -3 < 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124218 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9  + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124218 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124218 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} + z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nz^{2} &= 1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\\\\nz &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\ge 0; \\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\le 1\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 = 1\n$$\n\nand its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is of $z = z(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\bigg\\{(x, y) \\,:\\, \\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 \\leq 1 \\bigg\\}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124218 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9 =  1$ and its boundary.  \n\nTherefore, the domain is of $z = z(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\bigg\\{ (x, y) \\,:\\, \\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9 \\leq  1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124219 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}9 + \\dfrac{(y+4)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(z-5)^2}{25} = 1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124219 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}9 + \\dfrac{(y+4)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(z-5)^2}{25} = 1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124219 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 3)^{2})/(9) + ((0 + 4)^{2})/(16) - ((0 - 5)^{2})/(25) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(9) + 1 - 1 &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(9) &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nx - 3 &= ± \\sqrt{9} \\\\\nx &= 3 ± 3 \\\\\nx &= 0, 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124219 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(0+4)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(0-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+1-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =0,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124220 1071 "Identify the surface $z -1 = \\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}4$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124220 1071 "Identify the surface $z -1 = \\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}4.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124220 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nand a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a hyperbolic paraboloid with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n(x_0,y_0,z_0) = (3, -2, 1), \\quad a= b=2\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124220 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2},\n$$\n\nand a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is a hyperbolic paraboloid with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n(x_0,y_0,z_0) = (3, -2, 1), \\quad a= b=2.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124221 1071 "Identify the surface $x = \\dfrac{y^2}{4} - 4z^2$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124221 1071 "Identify the surface $x = \\dfrac{y^2}{4} - 4z^2.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124221 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $\\boldsymbol x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperbolic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis, and\n\n$$\nb=2, \\: c=\\dfrac 12\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124221 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $\\boldsymbol x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperbolic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis, and $b=2, \\: c=\\dfrac 12.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124222 1071 "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x^2y+9y$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124222 1071 "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x^2y+9y.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124222 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T\n$$\n\nor $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= 3x^{2} - 6xy = 3x(x - 2y) \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= 3y^{2} - 3x^{2} + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3x(x - 2y) & 3y^{2} - 3x^{2} + 9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nor $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nSetting\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nwe have the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x(x - 2y) = 0; 3y^{2} - 3x^{2} + 9 = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x=0$ or $x=2y$. Now, we substitute these values into the second equation.\n\n- For $x=0$, we get\n$3y^2 - 3(0)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y^2 = -3$,\nbut this last equation has no real solution.\n- For $x = 2y$, we obtain\n$3y^2 - 3(2y)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = \\pm 1$.\nSubstituting these values into $x = 2y$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y =-1 & ⟹x = 2(-1) =-2 \\\\ y = 1 & ⟹x = 2(1) = 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1)$.\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1)$. Note that since\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf 0^T\n$$\n\nat these two points, they are also both stationary points." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124222 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist, where\n![](images/db/db60be51a74c53caf3495a85e960be92ca8173c24c168e0ba30e335ef163be9e.png)\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy=3x(x-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n![](images/2f/2faec0235882f18aa523890be6f7f52f446219ba9a6c27fe8f8179ae90050d0d.png)\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$\n\nSetting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x(x-2y)=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x=0$ or $x=2y.$ Now, we substitute these values into the second equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(0)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y^2 = -3,\n$$\n\nbut this last equation has no real solution.\n\n- For $x = 2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(2y)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x = 2y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(-1)=-2 \\\\ y=1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf 0^T$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124223 1071 "The function\n$f(x,y) %= 2x + 2y - x^2 + y^2 + 2$\nhas the first derivative\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 2x & 2 + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand has a stationary point at $(1,-1)$. If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $H(1,-1) > 0$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) < 0$\n3. The point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124223 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y) \n%= 2x + 2y - x^2 + y^2 + 2$\nhas the first derivative\n![](images/08/083732de2321f70e27269ba55cec2a744a9424d443f68b4948caf313d30ae630.png)\nand has a stationary point at $(1,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,-1) > 0$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) <    0$\n\n- The point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186124223 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a)$, we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0$, then:if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$ if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0$, then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0$, then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 2x & 2 + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 - 2x) =-2 \\\\\n\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 + 2y) = 2 \\\\\n\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} &= \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} = \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 + 2y) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{″}(1,-1) = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)$, we get\n$H(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0$, the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186124223 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) <                0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) <       0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ![](images/d6/d680921af1b938e22a2705516c9f463cbb8b7bdb3ab1646b4613f6191e2a06f2.png) we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}