saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0$, then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 3x^2 - 3, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2y\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x^{2} - 3 = 0; 2y = 0\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n3x^2 -3= 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 1\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n2y = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=0\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y)$.\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} - 3) = 6x & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y) = 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x^{2} - 3) = 0 & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y) = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0)$, we obtain\n$f^{″}(1, 0) = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0)$, we get\n$H(1,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,0)) = 6 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = 12 > 0$.\nNow, since $H(1,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,0))_{11}= 6>0$, the function $f$ h