841 1074 "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n  a_{i}b_j = \\sum_{i=1}^m a_i \\sum_{j=1}^n b_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{18}({a}_{i}-2)({b}_{j}+1) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}({a}_{i}-2){\\sum }_{j=1}^{18}({b}_{j}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}({a}_{i}-2){\\sum }_{j=1}^{18}({b}_{j}+1) & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{a}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}2)\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{18}{b}_{j}+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{18}1) \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{a}_{i}-2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}1)\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{18}{b}_{j}+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{18}1) \\\\  & =(40-2\\cdot 15)\\cdot (-8+18) \\\\  & =(10)\\cdot (10) \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128841 1074 "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{i}b_j = \\sum_{i=1}^m a_i \\sum_{j=1}^n b_{j}\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(15)∑_(j = 1)^(18)(a_{i} - 2)(b_{j} + 1) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(15)(a_{i} - 2)∑_(j = 1)^(18)(b_{j} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(15)(a_{i} - 2)∑_(j = 1)^(18)(b_{j} + 1) &= (∑_(i = 1)^(15)a_{i} - ∑_(i = 1)^(15)2) \\cdot (∑_(j = 1)^(18)b_{j} + ∑_(j = 1)^(18)1) \\\\\n&= (∑_(i = 1)^(15)a_{i} - 2∑_(i = 1)^(15)1) \\cdot (∑_(j = 1)^(18)b_{j} + ∑_(j = 1)^(18)1) \\\\\n&= (40 - 2 \\cdot 15) \\cdot (-8 + 18) \\\\\n&= (10) \\cdot (10) \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128844 1071 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128844 1071 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/ee/eec66e1a6fdb16b157ec4ba8ea32b8e2328c26910594a7d21bb7346669797627.png)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](images/01/01df906c78b9ed5b4d6f1e29b4aeeb3be607b7570db357c4dce5949702432011.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128844 1074 "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(u,v) = v\\sin{u}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= -2v\\sin u & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|= 2\\cos u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= v\\cos u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= \\sin u\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2v\\sin u & 2\\cos u \\\\ v\\cos u & \\sin u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2v\\sin u \\cdot \\sin u - 2\\cos u \\cdot v\\cos u \\\\\n&=-2v(\\sin^{2} u + \\cos^{2} u) \\\\\n&=-2v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128844 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/62/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](images/72/72902f1a278bc1316ff58c54940b4d5e65fa04aa8e1711c8e0a1726f57cd3641.png) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128852 1071 "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$. The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$.\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0$.*" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128852 1071 "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n![](images/9e/9e6d498215d04524e5eec7343a1463a80182f3050eaf9ad5e4766cf7f5f50f14.png)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0.$*" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128852 1074 "For a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}$. So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}$.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad v(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad w(x,y,z) = yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} &= 2x & \\frac{∂u}{∂y}|= 0 & \\frac{∂u}{∂z}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x}|= y & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}|= x & \\frac{∂v}{∂z}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x}|= 0 & \\frac{∂w}{∂y}|= z & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}|= y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v, w))/(∂(x, y, z)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2x\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2x(xy - 0) \\\\\n&= 2x^{2}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}\n$$\n\nin terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2}y &= 2 \\cdot x \\cdot (xy) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{u} \\cdot v \\\\\n&= 2v\\sqrt{u}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= ((∂(u, v, w))/(∂(x, y, z)))^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128852 1074 "For a transformation \n\n![](images/8a/8a59f6bb056b614f0489a4fbfcba7c97ca6fed48d754863514a6e08db5767cf2.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad \nv(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad\nw(x,y,z) = yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v