86128856 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l$, respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(0,1)$ and the line through the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{(2 - 0)^{2} + (1 - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{l} &= 2.5 + 2^{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2.5 + 8 \\\\\n&= 10.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $10.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\cdot\\mathrm{m^2}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128856 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(0,1)$ and the line through the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-0)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =2.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $10.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\cdot\\mathrm{m^2}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128857 1071 "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda(x, y) =2(x^2+y^2)$, measured in $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2$, about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128857 1071 "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda(x, y) =2(x^2+y^2),$ measured in $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128857 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l$, respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y$, $D$'s center of mass is at $(0,0)$. The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = 2r^2\n$$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ) \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{1}2r^{2} \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}2r^{3}dr \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2π}dθ \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}r^{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\cdot \\left.θ\\right|_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2π \\\\\n&= πkg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{M} &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ) \\cdot r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{1}2r^{2} \\cdot r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}2r^{5}dr \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2π}dθ \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{3}r^{6}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\cdot \\left.θ\\right|_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2π \\\\\n&= \\frac{2π}{3}kg \\cdot m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= I_{M} + d^{2}M \\\\\n&= \\frac{2π}{3} + 1^{2} \\cdot π \\\\\n&= \\frac{2π}{3} + π \\\\\n&= \\frac{5π}{3}kg \\cdot m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128857 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ $D$'s center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda(r,\\theta) = 2r^2.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi \\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128859 1071 "Given that ![](images/f3/f3007b053a76b455c6c5b7ea617039780e6ee7b856ac2c54ae124dad334aef7f.png) express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n![](images/5c/5c67d939121c919b3d51b562efbc774a6f2a702d2a8e9cadee508446f9bd35d0.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128859 1071 "Given that $D = \\left\\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x^2 + (y+2)^2 \\leq 4\\right\\}$, express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D e^{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}A$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128859 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\, r \\, \\mathrm d r \\, \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D{:}$\n\n![](images/e4/e447e688e0f3e2c0411129e8d1baf4a900008173443f5392d98af52a27eb285b.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2)$.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{x = r\\cos θ; y = r\\sin θ\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the\n\n$$\nx^2 + (y+2)^2 = 4\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ + 2)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nr^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ + 4r\\sin θ + 4 &= 4 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) + 4r\\sin θ &= 0 \\\\\nr^{2} + 4r\\sin θ &= 0 \\\\\nr(r + 4\\sin θ) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have\n\n$$\nr = -4\\sin{\\theta}\n$$\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq -4\\sin{\\theta}, \\, \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)e^{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}dA &= ∬_(Δ)e^{\\sqrt{(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2}}}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{-4\\sin θ}re^{\\sqrt{r^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ)}}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{-4\\sin θ}re^{\\sqrt{r^{2}}}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{-4\\sin θ}re^{r}drdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128859 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n![](images/7f/7f645c475debdf46d06a9ecbebb3f019a7a92ea0c6129e06b8b5f326e005f9f6.png)\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region ![](images/c5/c592260e19248a715187208d54cafc6f4ceac8f34c843f1b366134bc37fb5430.png)\n\n![](images/e4/e447e688e0f3e2c0411129e8d1baf4a900008173443f5392d98af52a27eb285b.png)\n\nNotice that the region ![](images/33/33d4430552670d83a2c303e8ab286d3391f5611450f4e4f0b6ad6ccb58049b0e.png) represents the disk of radius ![](images/f7/f7b723906930985b61bc58989f341d6b2e1e1f70ff34946ce5f5f373e2b0f95a.png) centered at ![](images/48/484ceb02ac6fd60fc3d18cfff327672d25f5b88cb1b1825b8c1e069f153b9545.png) \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n![](images/00/00dbdb2d8638277969db40d2ca7c43266b4f275fbeadee86f6ef37954661433b.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ![](images/b7/b78de9de64536b59ded324af0c67d84d3d64802a8a8d1af3894abfc686e2ae53.png) in polar coordinates:\n![](images/18/185d596ce2824cf09bf7e5e7fd4cc432b75dfbe442c2e653564d93f347acf7ee.png) \n\nSince ![](images/42/427fd471dcf18cc90c67eb53f8f20e484fa707110281ad49811bc6a4f2ed5968.png) cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have ![](images/f5/f560ba12327ee0ad0c69dbd656ab82ac974acf442e8a1642dcf597f340d7f6db.png)\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for ![](images/42/427fd471dcf18cc90c67eb53f8f20e484fa707110281ad49811bc6a4f2ed5968.png) and ![](images/43/4302d6f15afc8636ab514cdce845f4bbe0171d743153ee6bdfbbe2b323bf4ac4.png) First of all, ![](images/15/15012c54ccf6548b6c3e2201e44d9ccfd33d7d41c627a0ce22c56f4c6d41d16c.png) can vary from ![](images/4e/4efe6d0d575bd5dd59ca5e6e05092a1d2030164be2ce65efd468d385e931fddb.png) (at the origin) to ![](images/ce/cecc5f8feb56bb745748bbc79f2bfdc884364e7b9dce2c027c8c251366bb6f32.png) (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/f5/f5ff820a0def58e4a2e6b15b5b77368151a71a72a083ef92fd95c20dacf711d3.png)\n\nSecondly, ![](images/e1/e10bbbc0bc5b9cc38adb59e4daa0cd709a3d7165da31914f73e17bf28cc01902.png) can vary between ![](images/a5/a55c0db211022b11f154d509c19b94022e218e9bf3120144dc2464bf5ca5c29c.png) (the negative ![](images/24/245bebfeb6cb95babe872c0185e33d598fb9dd08c8950252c07cdb5f5c8f87d4.png)-axis) and ![](images/bc/bc5b0c56a73784c1d673cae720a8d7796922204eb07796b954ff10b197fea2c1.png) (the positive ![](images/24/245bebfeb6cb95babe872c0185e33d598fb9dd08c8950252c07cdb5f5c8f87d4.png)-axis):\n\n![](images/c1/c1e5fea69b62b94b565607da8d372160cc51dc127973f2afa4dda8cfc01116ea.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates ![](images/7e/7eff2673c3d979c233082bd82d26977e80eec3963f1ff142a4f3a7c63ef89790.png) the region can be expressed as \n![](images/e2/e20685d2d0ab9822cb380f6e5ddf2cf41a24bcc1d5c28b903b77b1c660e347cd.png)\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n![](images/92/92792892be94c40c231d24839bf521ea22dce3d4fd809cfc46b7d4312ebd5d4d.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128860 1071 "By integrating first with respect to $z$, then $x$, and then $y$, evaluate the triple integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R z-xy \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the rectangular box $R = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\:0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq z \\leq 3 \\right\\}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128860 1071 "By integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y,$ evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R z-xy  \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the rectangular box $R = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq z \\leq 3 \\right\\}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128860 1074 "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} z-xy \\: \\textrm{d}V\n= \n\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{1}^{3} z-xy \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nSince we're integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y,$ we write $\\textrm d V = \\textrm d z\\:\\textrm d x\\:\\textrm d y.$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}z-xy\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}z-xy\\,dz]dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}-xyz]}_{z=1}^{z=3}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}(9-1)-xy(3-1)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-2xy\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-2xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1