eft\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 \\cos^2{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{y} &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ) \\cdot r^{3}\\cos^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/6}^{π/4}\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ} \\cdot r^{3}\\cos^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{π/6}^{π/4}\\int_{1}^{2}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= (\\int_{1}^{2}rdr)(\\int_{π/6}^{π/4}dθ) \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}r^{2}\\right|_{1}^{2} \\cdot \\left.θ\\right|_{π/6}^{π/4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4 - 1) \\cdot (\\frac{π}{4} - \\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\frac{π}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128869 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(r,\\theta),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_x = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\sin^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3\\cos^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 \\cos^2{\\theta}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligne