orem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l$, respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{3}8\\,\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{0+2}2, \\dfrac{0+4}2 \\right) = (1,2)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{M} &= ∬_(D)((x - x^(¯))^{2} + (y - y^(¯))^{2})λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{8}((x - 1)^{2} + (y - 2)^{2})dydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{4}3(x - 1)^{2} + 3(y - 2)^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\\int_{0}^{2}[3(x - 1)^{2}y + (y - 2)^{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\\int_{0}^{2}12(x - 1)^{2} + 16dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}[4(x - 1)^{3} + 16x]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}(4 + 32 - (-4 + 0)) \\\\\n&= 5kg \\cdot m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= I_{M} + d^{2}M \\\\\n&= 5 + 1^{2} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 5 + 3 \\\\\n&= 8kg \\cdot m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128871 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,