 of mass, denoted $\\overline{x}$, of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}m_{1} &= 2, & m_{2}|= 4, & m_{3}|= 3, \\\\ x_{1}|=-1, & x_{2}|= 2, & x_{3}|= 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)m_{i} \\\\\n&= m_{1} + m_{2} + m_{3} \\\\\n&= 2 + 4 + 3 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{O} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)m_{i}x_{i} \\\\\n&= m_{1}x_{1} + m_{2}x_{2} + m_{3}x_{3} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-1) + 4 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&=-2 + 8 + 12 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{18}{9}=2\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128873 1074 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overline{x},$ of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, & \\,{m}_{3} & =3, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+4+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+8+12 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{18}{9}=2.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128874 1071 "![](images/0e/0e4802c6642fc0950358235f30fb8c994d26b912e557122af08b99277bb6192a.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$, what is the value of $\\overline{y}$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128874 1071 "![](images/0e/0e4802c6642fc0950358235f30fb8c994d26b912e557122af08b99277bb6192a.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overline{y}?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128874 1074 "The center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ on the plane is given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 1\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 3, \\qquad y_2 = 0, \\qquad y_3 = 3\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)m_{i} \\\\\n&= m_{1} + m_{2} + m_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 + 5 + 1 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{x} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)m_{i}y_{i} \\\\\n&= m_{1}y_{1} + m_{2}y_{2} + m_{3}y_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 9 + 0 + 3 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{12}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128874 1074 "The center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 3, \\qquad y_2 = 0, \\qquad y_3 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9+0+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{12}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128875 1071 "![](images/44/440f58e3d5e652374689083ffa672f505888259a51e1c5ef2207d94410eb8cab.png)\n\n     \n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $10\\,\\rm{lb}$ and is $4\\,\\rm{ft}$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186128875 1071 "![](images/44/440f58e3d5e652374689083ffa672f505888259a51e1c5ef2207d94410eb8cab.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $10\\,\\rm{lb}$ and is $4\\,\\rm{ft}$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128875 1074 "Let $m$ be the mass of $A$.\nThe length of the rod is $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $4\\,\\rm{ft}$. So, the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is\n\n$$\n9 - 4 = 5\\,\\rm{ft}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc2503e1ed1cd91a9f3876ec5735d5c375c36b9bcac6216659a29f5efa294ea0.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-m \\cdot 5 = -5m\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $10 \\cdot 4 = 40\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40 - 5m &= 0 \\\\\n5m &= 40 \\\\\nm &= 8lb\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186128875 1074 "Let $m$ be the mass of $A.$\nThe length of the rod is $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $4\\,\\rm{ft}.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is\n\n$$\n9 - 4 = 5\\,\\rm{ft},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc2503e1ed1cd91a9f3876ec5735d5c375c36b9bcac6216659a29f5efa294ea0.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-m \\cdot 5 = -5m\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $10 \\cdot 4 = 40\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40-5m & =0 \\\\ 5m & =40 \\\\ m & =8\\,lb.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133472 1071 "Find $5\\det(A)-\\det(B)$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\sqrt{13} & 4 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 9 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133472 1071 "Find $5\\det(A)-\\det(B),$ where \n![](images/65/65df227e4fff9e934cec11be0a0262a1093a82270f3c9b0b7970fa2c700151aa.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133472 1074 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n![](images/23/23865048a3c2ad60dd3b51fe78c44ee368692172300814c3fb061f447266e233.png)\n\n- Since $B$ is a diagonal matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\det(B) = 3 \\cdot (-4) \\cdot (-2) = 24\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\det(A)-\\det(B) = 5 \\cdot 0 - 24 = -24.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133472 1074 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\sqrt{13} & 4 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 9 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- Since $B$ is a diagonal matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal:\n$\\det(B) = 3 \\cdot (-4) \\cdot (-2) = 24$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5\\det(A)-\\det(B) = 5 \\cdot 0 - 24 = -24\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133473 1071 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133473 1071 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n![](images/d9/d95e60cbfa50178878e719166ace3dbff90c3cc5531acfc046d8354320cd4b00.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133473 1074 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has proportional rows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nAs a result, $\\mid A \\mid = 0$.\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, so\n$\\mid B \\mid = (-1) \\cdot 4 \\cdot (-3) = 12 \\neq 0$.\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero row:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nAs a result, $\\mid C \\mid = 0$.\n\nTherefore, only $\\mid A \\mid$ and $\\mid C \\mid$ are equal to $0$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133473 1074 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has proportional rows: \n![](images/3f/3f434912c97c20d04f703253fe41c169497cae82657cfbd0d8b548985549fe64.png)\nAs a result, $|A| = 0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, so \n\n$$\n|B| = (-1) \\cdot 4 \\cdot (-3) = 12 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero row: \n![](images/5f/5f41ff4f7fe5aeae5865bef9ab16d5d97a3513277f70b797c4167882093ed079.png)\nAs a result, $|C| = 0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133474 1071 "![](images/cc/ccc50eec6218625f22e8372d6f0b1dbf0da0aa921dff237e4e1b8ea3761bdfc7.png)\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0.$\n\n- $A$ has a zero column\n\n- $A$ has proportional rows\n\n- $A$ has identical columns" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133474 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matr