e integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 4x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}V\n= \n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}} 4x^3 \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}4{x}^{3}\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}[{x}^{4}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{(\\sqrt{y})}^{4}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c8712fa0a30ad94f503efe5e080ee89f83eb21f19fba45033bbedeab08ac816.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{y}^{2}z]_{z=0}^{z=4-y}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{2}(4-y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}4{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(64-0)-\\frac{1}{4}(256-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133481 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 4x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}} 4x^3 \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\right] \\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}4x^{3}dx]dA &= ∬_(D)[x^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = \\sqrt{y}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\sqrt{y})^{4}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)y^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c8712fa0a30ad94f503efe5e080ee89f83eb21f19fba45033bbedeab08ac816.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)y^{2}dA &= \\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{0}^{4 - y}y^{2}dzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}[\\int_{0}^{4 - y}y^{2}dz]dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}[y^{2}z]_{z = 0}^{z = 4 - y}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}y^{2}(4 - y)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}4y^{2} - y^{3}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{4}{3}y^{3} - \\frac{1}{4}y^{4}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}(64 - 0) - \\frac{1}{4}(256 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133482 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{0}^{z} \\dfrac{\\cos (2y)}{1-y} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (y,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2\\sqrt{1-y} \\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133482 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{z} \\dfrac{\\cos (2y)}{1-y} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}A$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n$D = \\left\\{(y,z) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2\\sqrt{1-y} \\right\\}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133482 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)[\\int_{0}^{z}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dx]dA &= ∬_(D)(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)[x]_{x = 0}^{x = z}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)(z - 0)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dA &= \\int_{0}^{π/6}\\int_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1 - y}}(z\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)dzdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/6}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)[\\frac{z^{2}}{2}]_{z = 0}^{z = 2\\sqrt{1 - y}}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/6}(\\cos (2y))/(1 - y)((4(1 - y))/(2) - 0)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/6}2\\cos (2y)dy \\\\\n&= [\\sin (2y)]_{y = 0}^{y = π/6} \\\\\n&= \\sin (\\frac{π}{3}) - \\sin (0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133482 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}[x]_{x=0}^{x=z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}(z-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-y}}\\frac{z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}{[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2\\sqrt{1-y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}{1-y}(\\frac{4(1-y)}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133483 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2$. According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133483 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2.$ According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133483 1074 "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](images/02/02c989a33b6f027c0814d39d1468379dcb24f90536257b2b0bf4031191a73f85.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $