x^{2}ydV \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}6x^{2}ydzdydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}3x^{2}dx \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2}2ydy \\cdot \\int_{1}^{2}dz \\\\\n&= [x^{3}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\cdot [y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 2} \\cdot (2 - 1) \\\\\n&= (1 - 0) \\cdot (4 - 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133497 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133509 1071 "![](images/f6/f6fb8d85c6e0921d49f16b410a3c3d7c549caabb166b0e65bb4c1982844310c3.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a square matrix\n\n- $A \\cdot A^T = I$\n\n- $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133509 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is a square matrix\n2. $A \\cdot A^T = I$\n3. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133509 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^T = A^T\\cdot A = I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is true. Since $A\\cdot A^T = I$, our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133509 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^T = A^T\\cdot A = I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n![](images/3e/3e419059afe53ef90b1c086f75c29513e7f6cc269ac4ecbf16e0db919222b3f5.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A\\cdot A^T = I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133510 1071 "![](images/08/08c339defb7800dfbafef33db26281a6eb44740a61ff9d47bae8e9adc6af5f75.png)\n\nFind $|\\textrm{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133510 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind $\\mid \\textrm{det}(PQ) \\mid$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133510 1074 "We use the following facts:\n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm1$.\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}det(PQ) & &= & det(P) \\cdot det(Q)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid (± 1) \\cdot det(Q) \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}± 1 & \\cdot & det(Q)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid det(Q) \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid (-2\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 18 - 2 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 20 \\mid \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133510 1074 "We use the following facts: \n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm1.$\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\text{det}(PQ)| & =|\\text{det}(P)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(\\pm 1)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\pm 1|\\cdot |\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(-2\\sqrt{3})\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}-2\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =|-18-2| \\\\  & =|-20| \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133511 1071 "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix ![](images/0f/0fdcc6a3e329666b00a45c05292d85ab2df6366ff38d5506233b6c15064d2149.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133511 1071 "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133511 1074 "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD^{-1} &= D^{T} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133511 1074 "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133512 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q$?\n\n1. $2Q$ is orthogonal\n2. $P^{-1}Q$ is orthogonal\n3. $PQ^T$ is orthogonal" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133512 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\n- $2Q$ is orthogonal\n\n- $P^{-1}Q$ is orthogonal\n\n- $PQ^T$ is orthogonal" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186133512 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ Q^T = Q^T Q = I\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q$, we have $Q^{-1} = Q^T$.\n- $(AB)^T = B^TA^T$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $2Q \\cdot (2Q)^T$, we obtain\n$2Q \\cdot (2Q)^{T}|= 2Q \\cdot 2Q^{T}; = 4QQ^{T}; = 4I; \\ne I$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(P^{-1}Q)\\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^T$, we obtain\n$(P^{-1}Q) \\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^{T}|= (P^{T}Q) \\cdot (P^{T}Q)^{T}; = P^{T}Q \\cdot Q^{T}(P^{T})^{T}; = P^{T}(QQ^{T})P; = P^{T}(I)P; = P^{T}P; = I$.\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(PQ^T)\\cdot(PQ^T)^T$, we obtain\n$(PQ^{T}) \\cdot (PQ^{T})^{T}|= PQ^{T} \\cdot (Q^{T})^{T}P^{T}; = PQ^{T}QP^{T}; = P(Q^{T}Q)P^{T}; = P(I)P^{T}; = PP^{T}; = I$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186133512 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ Q^T = Q^T Q = I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have $Q^{-1} = Q^T.$\n\n- $(AB)^T = B^TA^T$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $2Q \\cdot (2Q)^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T} & =2Q\\cdot 2{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(P^{-1}Q)\\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({P}^{-1}Q)\\cdot ({P}^{-1}Q)^{T} & =({P}^{T}Q)\\cdot ({P}^{T}Q)^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}Q\\cdot {Q}^{T}({P}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}(Q{Q}^{T})P \\\\  & ={P}^{T}(I)P \\\\  & ={P}^{T}P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(PQ^T)\\cdot(PQ^T)^T ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P{Q}^{T})\\cdot (P{Q}^{T})^{T} & =P{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{Q}^{T}Q{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P({Q}^{T}Q){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186151883 1071 "Find the least common multiple of $4(x+1)$ and $6(x+1)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186151883 1071 "Find the least common multiple of $4(x+1)$ and $6(x+1).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186151883 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)$:\n\n- Multiples of $4$: $\\quad 4, \\, 8, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $6$: $\\quad 6, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\, 18, \\ldots$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(4,6) = {\\color{blue}{12}}\n$$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+1), \\, (x+1)\\right) = {\\color{red}{(x+1)}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(4(x+1), \\, 6(x+1)\\right) = {\\color{blue}{12}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x+1)}} = 12(x+1)\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186151883 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $4\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 4, \\, 8, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $6\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 6, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\, 18, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(4,6) =  {\\color{blue}{12}}.$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+1), \\, (x+1)\\right) = {\\color{red}{(x+1)}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(4(x+1), \\, 6(x+1)\\right) = {\\color{blue}{12}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x+1)}} = 12(x+1).\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186151885 1071 "Find the least common multiple of $9(x-1)^4$ and $12(x-1)^2$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186151885 1071 "Find the least common multiple of $9(x-1)^4$ and $12(x-1)^2.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186151885 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(9,12)$:\n\n- Multiples of $9$: $\\q