$f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157003 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x) = x \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nis an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\sin (-x) \\\\\n&=-x(-\\sin x) \\\\\n&= x\\sin x \\\\\n&= f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because\n\n$$\nf(x) = x \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nis an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157004 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157004 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157004 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157004 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n$f(-x)|= 4(-x)^{3}; =-4x^{3}; =-f(x)✓$\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0$.\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n$g(-x)|= e^{(-x)} + e^{- (-x)}; = e^{-x} + e^{x}; = e^{x} + e^{-x}; \\ne - g(x)\\cdot$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157005 1071 "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A$, what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157005 1071 "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157005 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157005 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x) = x\\cos x\n$$\n\nis an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\cos (-x) \\\\\n&=-x\\cos x \\\\\n&= - (x\\cos x) \\\\\n&=-f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because\n\n$$\nf(x) = x\\cos x\n$$\n\nis an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-1}^{1}x\\cos xdx &= 0 \\\\\n\\int_{-1}^{0}x\\cos xdx + \\int_{0}^{1}x\\cos xdx &= 0 \\\\\n\\int_{-1}^{0}x\\cos xdx + A &= 0 \\\\\n\\int_{-1}^{0}x\\cos xdx &= -A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157006 1071 "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(\\pi,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n![](images/08/08502aa81729d4f88aec094e2e2f5c7b4a57825ef3767f1eec29700ceb6e21a3.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157006 1071 "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(\\pi,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{y}\\cos x & e^{y}\\sin x\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157006 1074 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{y}\\cos x & e^{y}\\sin x\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = e^y\\cos x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =e^y\\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(e^{y}\\cos x) =-e^{y}\\sin x & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(e^{y}\\sin x) = e^{y}\\cos x \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(e^{y}\\cos x) = e^{y}\\cos x & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(e^{y}\\sin x) = e^{y}\\sin x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-e^{y}\\sin x & e^{y}\\cos x \\\\ e^{y}\\cos x & e^{y}\\sin x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(\\pi,0)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157006 1074 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n![](images/02/025dd1a0b89b52e32a3e42e5bd3492261ed060f104f627051b6950c7f94d66c1.png)\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/f1/f1a47569220f01e1dc474a13a5040e969449ee0b10cfe7df39c3aec4c6175f41.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n![](images/f0/f0b46d26ccc6fefc404b021bde11c4d6959c00ae0ef1145a9984da602bcc54ef.png)\n\nFirst, notice that since ![](images/96/964f8ac9ca9c305faef8562f66d063f318ecbe402df337377bd9efdf61301567.png) \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = e^y\\cos x ,  \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =e^y\\sin x .\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(\\pi,0).$ This gives\n\n![](images/54/540c920abd2989b29aae275eedd179c5118e82354915e591c0d1c5296ca9fa21.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157007 1071 "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix}$\nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157007 1071 "Given that \n![](images/b3/b3e96b1a54af842b69da4ae5fcb44858bffdac48b27cde6677c15c05906e890d.png) \n evaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157007 1074 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(\\mathbf x) = \\det \\left(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x) \\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\ln y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac xy\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln y) = 0 & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) = \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln y) = \\frac{1}{y} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) =-\\frac{x}{y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{″}(0, 1) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1) = 0 \\cdot 0 - 1\\cdot 1 = -1\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157007 1074 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n![](images/af/af5524049f5f8b3c4c4241520f2d874b454d61b1b025362f5193437f766e610b.png)\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/88/8882516e2732e419efdfb8439265212dd4f380d6a6ea58f6ff1291030bf50bfb.png)\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(\\mathbf x) = \\det \\left( \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x) \\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n![](images/4f/4fede2bb5c2306f62b55b7996488baee52c69e7d41b011e0e9d2b7405e63a8f4.png)\n\nFirst, notice that since ![](images/92/9252d1ce4f5df828f45351d01e0709cf2f2ca5df2c9c25390c111f40ca00fb22.png) we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\ln y, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1) = 0 \\cdot 0 - 1\\cdot 1 = -1.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157008 1071 "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)  = x^2 e^y.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186157008 1071 "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y) = x^2 e^y$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186157008 1074 "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative 