\\\\ 0 & -2 - λ & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 - λ & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)((-2 - λ)(-2 - λ) - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)(λ^{2} + 4λ + 3) &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)(λ + 3)(λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 1,-1,-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = -3\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186166499 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/d7/d7716e2c2924212e880275dd674d4a951daf66ac41e5a888a57acb6be686118f.png)\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-1,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = -3.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186171716 1071 "![](images/6a/6ad2702c610c5e7d31d07584c6e605467ff25c8474befd433214451169cc1180.png)\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z) = z-y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathb