peratorname{cos}⁡z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186171746 1074 "Let $z$ be a $6$th root of unity. This means that\n\n$$\nz^{6} = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\mid 1 \\mid = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\arg(1) = 0\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\nz^{6} = e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}}⟹z = (e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}})^{1/6} = e^{kπ\\textrm{i}/3}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1$, and is therefore equal to $e^{\\pi \\textrm{i}/3}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186171746 1074 "Let $z$ be a $6$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\nz^{6} = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}.$ Notice that $|1| = 1$ and $\\arg(1) = 0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to $e^{\\pi \\textrm{i}/3}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186171747 1074 "Geometrically, the $4$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a square (regular $4$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/91/9111f21143412d791b0195722da4f6d461444f386efd9baf609c70a4fe709fd8.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186171747 1074 "Geometrically, the $4$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n- one of the roots is $1$, corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n- the roots form a square (regular $4$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/91/9111f21143412d791b0195722da4f6d461444f386efd9baf609c70a4fe709fd8.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186171748 1074 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0)$.\n\nSince there are $7$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $7$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\mid 1 \\mid = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\arg(1) = 0\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\nz^{7} = e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}}⟹z = (e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}})^{1/7} = e^{2kπ\\textrm{i}/7}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/73/730b0c3c63799b9101ce89d38e3d96c10bc0e217b967506393ea67757ecf48d7.png)\n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5$. Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nz_{5} = e^{2(5)π\\textrm{i}/7} = e^{10π\\textrm{i}/7}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186171748 1074 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $7$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $7$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}.$ Notice that $|1| = 1$ and $\\arg(1) = 0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/73/730b0c3c63799b9101ce89d38e3d96c10bc0e217b967506393ea67757ecf48d7.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{5}={e}^{2(5)\\pi \\text{i}/7}={e}^{10\\pi \\text{i}/7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186175699 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\cos x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n1. The range of the function is $[-1,1]$.\n2. The function has minimum values at $x =n\\pi$.\n3. The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +\\pi n$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186175699 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\cos x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The range of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function has minimum values at $x =n\\pi.$\n\n- The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +\\pi n.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186175699 1074 "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos{x}$.\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The minimum value is $-1$, and the maximum value is $1$. Therefore, the range is $[-1,1]$.\n- Statement II is false. The function has a minimum at $x=\\pi$ and the period is $2\\pi$. Therefore, the minimum values occur at $\\pi+2n\\pi$.\n- Statement III is true. These are the zeros of the cosine curve. The cosine curve has zeros at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186175699 1074 "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos{x}.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$ Therefore, the range is $[-1,1].$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a minimum at $x=\\pi$ and the period is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, the minimum values occur at $\\pi+2n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement III is true. These are the zeros of the cosine curve. The cosine curve has zeros at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186175700 1071 "If $\\cos x =- \\dfrac {1}{3},$ find the value of $2\\cos( x + 4\\pi)+3.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186175700 1071 "If $\\cos x =-\\dfrac {1}{3}$, find the value of $2\\cos(x + 4\\pi)+3$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186175700 1074 "The period of $y = \\cos x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\cos{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos x &= \\cos (x + 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\cos (x + 4π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} =-\\dfrac {1}{3}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\cos{(x+4\\pi)} =-\\dfrac {1}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos (x + 4π) + 3 &= 2(-\\frac{1}{3}) + 3 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3} + 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186175700 1074 "The period of $y = \\cos x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\cos{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x & =cos⁡(x+2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =cos⁡(x+4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cos{x} =- \\dfrac {1}{3},$ then $\\cos{(x+4\\pi)} = - \\dfrac {1}{3}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2cos⁡(x+4\\pi )+3 & =2(-\\frac{1}{3})+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186175701 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n1. The zeros of the function are at $x = \\pi n$.\n2. The function has asymptotes at $x = 2\\pi n$.\n3. The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1]$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186175701 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The zeros of the function are at $x = \\pi n.$\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x = 2\\pi n.$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1].$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186175701 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan x$.\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm2\\pi,\\ldots$, which can be written as $x=n\\pi$.\n- Statement II is false. The function has an asymptote at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2$ and the period is $\\pi$. Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2 + \\pi n$.\n- Statement III is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186175701 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm2\\pi,\\ldots,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has an asymptote at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2$ and the period is $\\pi.$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2 + \\pi n