\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡x & =cot⁡(x+5\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =cot⁡(x+5\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\cot{x} = 1,$ then $\\cot{(x+5\\pi)} = 1.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186175709 1071 "If $\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}$, then find the value of $\\cos (-x-6\\pi)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186175709 1071 "If $\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4},$ then find the value of $\\cos (-x-6\\pi).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186175709 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\cos{x}$.\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\cos{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\cos{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n$\\cos(-x) = \\cos{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\cos{x}$, we have\n$\\cos (-x - 6π)|= \\cos (-x - 2π \\cdot 3); = \\cos (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\cos{x}$, we have\n$\\cos (-x)|= \\cos x; = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos (-x-6\\pi)= \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186175709 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\cos{x}.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\cos{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\cos{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(-x) = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\cos{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x-6\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 3) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\cos{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos (-x-6\\pi)= \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}.$" 