lse] [17592186179446 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m},$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186179446 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)=t^2-2t+3,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+3) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) =  2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s},\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186179446 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^2-2t+3\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - 2t + 3) \\\\\n&= 2t - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) = 2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ΔKE \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot (4)^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0^{2} \\\\\n&= 8 - 0 \\\\\n&= 8J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186179447 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2$, calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186179447 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186179447 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+3x]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+12)-(2+6) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 12 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186179447 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= \\int_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}F(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{2}^{4}(x + 3)dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + 3x]_{2}^{4} \\\\\n&= (8 + 12) - (2 + 6) \\\\\n&= 12J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity\n\n$$\nv=2\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nat $x=2$. So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= \\frac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2} \\\\\n12 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot v_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2^{2} \\\\\n12 &= v_{1}^{2} - 4 \\\\\nv_{1}^{2} &= 16 \\\\\nv_{1} &= ± 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration.\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183920 1071 "Solve the equation $-2x^2 - 4x = 1$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^2 + 2x + 1$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183920 1071 "Solve the equation $-2x^2 - 4x = 1.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^2 + 2x + 1$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183920 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\nx^2 + 2x + 1 = (x + 1)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x^{2} - 4x &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x^{2} + 2x) &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1x) &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1x + 1^{2} - 1^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n- 2[(x + 1)^{2} - 1^{2}] &= 1 \\\\\n- 2[(x + 1)^{2} - 1] &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x + 1)^{2} + (-2)(-1) &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x + 1)^{2} + 2 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $2$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-2)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(x + 1)^{2} + 2 &= 1 \\\\\n- 2(x + 1)^{2} + 2 - 2 &= 1 - 2 \\\\\n- 2(x + 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} &= ((-1))/((-2)) \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\nx + 1 &= ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\nx &= -1 ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183920 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\nx^2 + 2x + 1 = (x + 1)^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-4x & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2x) & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x) & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =1 \\\\ -2[(x+1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ -2[(x+1)^{2}-1] & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+(-2)(-1) & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+2 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $2$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(x+1)^{2}+2 & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+2-2 & =1-2 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(-2)} \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ |x+1| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183924 1071 "![](images/5c/5c84e8bf0c80a5c57252ff368d0f59499b0299d4e59f49fdf738e3869f0bcf4f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan(Bx) + D$, where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B + D$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183924 1071 "![](images/5c/5c84e8bf0c80a5c57252ff368d0f59499b0299d4e59f49fdf738e3869f0bcf4f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan(Bx) + D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B + D?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183924 1074 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{5} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y = A\\tan(Bx + C) + D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B} \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. The graph shows that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D = 1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB + D =\\dfrac{5}{2} +1 = \\dfrac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny = \\tan\\left(\\frac{5x}{2} \\right) +1.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183924 1074 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function\n\n$$\ny = A\\tan(Bx + C) + D\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B} \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. The graph shows that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D = 1$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB + D =\\dfrac{5}{2} +1 = \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny = \\tan\\left(\\frac{5x}{2} \\right) +1\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183927 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183927 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183927 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos(x){:}\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1]$.\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)((\\arccos (x) - π)/(x - π)) &= (\\arccos (0) - π)/(0 - π) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{π}{2} - π))/((- π)) \\\\\n&= ((-\\frac{π}{2}))/((- π)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183927 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\arccos(x){:}$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi }{x-\\pi }) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)-\\pi }{0-\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi )}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183928 1071 "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183928 1071 "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)?$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183928 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\arccos(x-1){:}\n$$\n\n![](images/4c/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2]$. This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183928 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y = \\arccos(x-1){:}$\n\n![](images/4c/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2].$ This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183929 1071 "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183929 1071 "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183929 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}\n$$\n\n![](images/06/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)\n$$\n\nis $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty$, the graph tends to\n\n$$\ny={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)\\cos (\\frac{2}{3}\\arctan (2x + 1)) &= \\cos (\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183929 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/06/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph tends to $y={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183944 1074 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & <4 \\\\ 2y & <-x+4 \\\\ y & <-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny < -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](images/14/1450257c533c670d3ce0d803aa62b75c2ffe44159e26a3daf5dd3c04026a6fe4.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183944 1074 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 2y & < 4 \\\\ 2y & < - x + 4 \\\\ y & < - \\frac{1}{2}x + 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nitself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality\n\n$$\ny < -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nso we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](images/14/1450257c533c670d3ce0d803aa62b75c2ffe44159e26a3daf5dd3c04026a6fe4.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183945 1074 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-y & \\le -1 \\\\ -y & \\le x-1 \\\\ y & \\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-x+1.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-x+1$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a *solid line*.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny \\geq -x+1\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line $y=-x+1,$ so we shade *above* the line. \n\n![](images/a6/a649958285f52e9d61c665bf4cd58844e4ed7cea1cf9e0f949efe5a0f25e3263.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183945 1074 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-x - y & \\le - 1 \\\\ -y & \\le x - 1 \\\\ y & \\ge - x + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line\n\n$$\ny=-x+1\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+1\n$$\n\nitself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a *solid line*.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality\n\n$$\ny \\geq -x+1\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+1\n$$\n\nso we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](images/a6/a649958285f52e9d61c665bf4cd58844e4ed7cea1cf9e0f949efe5a0f25e3263.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183947 1074 "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0)$. Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-1$.\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use.\n\n- Since the line is *dashed*, we will use a *strict* inequality.\n- Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n$x > -1$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183947 1074 "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation \n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. \n\n- Since the line is *dashed*, we will use a *strict* inequality.\n\n- Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx > -1.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183949 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n$-(x^2 -7x +13)$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 -7x+13\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x +{\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwriting the constant term ${\\color{purple}{13}}$ 