n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x +  {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{2}) & =2({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183951 1071 "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int x^2 e^{x^3+1}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183951 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int x^2 e^{x^3+1}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183951 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable\n\n$$\nu = x^3+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm{d}u = x^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intx^{2}e^{x^{3} + 1}dx &= \\inte^{x^{3} + 1} \\cdot x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\inte^{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\inte^{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{u} + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{x^{3} + 1} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 fals