nt {3^{u}}  \\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=  -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\cdot\\dfrac{3^{u}}{\\ln 3} + C\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=   -\\dfrac{3^{1-x^4}}{4\\ln 3}+ C.\n}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183953 1071 "Calculate  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {e^{2x}} {e^{2x} + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183953 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {e^{2x}} {e^{2x} + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183953 1074 "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable\n\n$$\nu = e^{2x}+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 2e^{2x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2}\\, \\textrm{d}u = e^{2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{e^{2x}}{e^{2x} + 1}dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{e^{2x} + 1} \\cdot e^{2x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}u & + C \\\\ = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln & e^{2x} + 1\\end{vmatrix}+ C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (e^{2x} + 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that $e^{2x} + 1 > 0$ for all $x$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183953 1074 "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u = e^{2x}+1.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 2e^{2x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\, \\textrm{d}u = e^{2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{e}^{2x}+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x}+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that $e^{2x} + 1 > 0$ for all $x.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183954 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt {\\ln 4}} 16xe^{-2x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183954 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt {\\ln 4}} 16xe^{-2x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186183954 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u=-2x^2$. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-4x \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad -4 \\, \\textrm{d}u=16x \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the new limits of integration:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt {\\ln 4}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-2\\ln 4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\ln 4}}16xe^{-2x^{2}}dx &= \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\ln 4}}e^{-2x^{2}} \\cdot 16xdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{-2\\ln 4}e^{u} \\cdot (-4du) \\\\\n&=-4\\int_{0}^{-2\\ln 4}e^{u}du \\\\\n&= \\left.- 4e^{u}\\right|_{0}^{-2\\ln 4} \\\\\n&=-4(e^{-2\\ln 4} - e^{0}) \\\\\n&=-4(\\frac{1}{16} - 1) \\\\\n&= 4(\\frac{15}{16}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186183954 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u=-2x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-4x\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n-4 \\, \\textrm{d}u=16x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the new limits of integration:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt {\\ln 4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-2\\ln 4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}16x{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\cdot 16x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\cdot (-4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-4({e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{16}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{15}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186187992 1071 "![](images/bd/bdeef265fec0703b42b8d04e55502da49e8e84624a761368edd1dd5c106c7dbc.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r =-2 \\cos\\theta$, shown above." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186187992 1071 "![](images/bd/bdeef265fec0703b42b8d04e55502da49e8e84624a761368edd1dd5c106c7dbc.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r =-2 \\cos\\theta,$ shown above." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186187992 1074 "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nr=-2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -2\\cos θ\\cos θ =-2\\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= -2\\cos θ\\sin θ =-\\sin 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)$.\n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$x^{′}(θ)|= (-2\\cos^{2} θ)^{′}; =-2 \\cdot 2\\cos θ \\cdot (-\\sin θ); = 4\\sin θ\\cos θ; = 2\\sin 2θ$\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$y^{′}(θ)|= (-\\sin 2θ)^{′}; = - (\\cos 2θ \\cdot 2); =-2\\cos 2θ$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2\\cos 2θ}{2\\sin 2θ} \\\\\n&=-\\cot 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186187992 1074 "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186192433 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186192433 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186192433 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186192433 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(\\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{∞}\\frac{6}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= 6 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 2^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[\\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{2})]_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\\n&= 3lim_(a → ∞)(\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2}) - \\arctan (\\sqrt{3})) \\\\\n&= 3lim_(a → ∞)(\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2}) - \\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 3lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2})] - π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{∞}\\frac{6}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= 3lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2})] - π \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2} - π \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186192434 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186192434 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186192434 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-1/4}^{∞}\\frac{2}{1 + 16x^{2}}dx &= 2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1 + 16x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{-1/4}^{a}(1)/(1 + (4x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (4x)]_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)(\\arctan (4a) - \\arctan (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (4a)] - (-\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (4a)] + \\frac{π}{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan{(4x)}\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](images/9a/9a7272f8bc8d0eba3e5bfc38e843cd02db3f3eb7b51e77b95b2efd61034890b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-1/4}^{∞}\\frac{2}{1 + 16x^{2}}dx &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (4a)] + \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (\\frac{π}{2} + \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186192434 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{ar