$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}\\int_{1}^{∞}2^{-u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{1}^{a}2^{-u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[- \\frac{2^{-u}}{\\ln 2}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[2^{-u}]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)\\frac{1}{2^{a}} - \\frac{1}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2^x}\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0$\n\n![](images/19/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{∞}x^{3} \\cdot 2^{-x^{4}}dx &= -\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)\\frac{1}{2^{a}} - \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4\\ln 2} \\cdot (0 - \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8\\ln 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186192435 1074 "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {li