{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A$.\nDetermine the missing expression." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198049 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,x,-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = 0, \\qquad\nQ = x, \\qquad\nR = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198049 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1$. Since the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle$. As a result, we have\n$∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot ndS; = ∬_(D)⟨0, x,-z^{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩dA; = ∬_(D) - z^{2}dA; = ∬_(D)0dA; = 0$.\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2$.We compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂h}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}}; \\frac{∂h}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}}$\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n$P = 0, \\qquad Q = x, \\qquad R = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4$.\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS|= - ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y} + R)dA; = - ∬_(D)(-0 \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} - x \\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} + x^{2} + y^{2} - 4)dA; = ∬_(D)\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} - x^{2} - y^{2} + 4dA$.\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x y 4 - x 2 - y 2 - x 2 - y 2 + 4dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198050 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\, y, \\, 0 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinder $z=1-x^2$ and the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$. Let $D$ be