}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y , \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (2x)-y\\cdot (2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198050 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1$.We compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂g}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1 - x^{2}) =-2x; \\frac{∂g}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1 - x^{2}) = 0$\n\n> If we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n> $P = x, \\qquad Q = y, \\qquad R = 0$.\n> As a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n> $∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA; = ∬_(D)(-x \\cdot (-2x) - y \\cdot (0) + 0)dA; = ∬_(D)2x^{2}dA$.\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2$. We compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂h}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2x; \\frac{∂h}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2y$\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n$P = x, \\qquad Q = y, \\qquad R = 0$.\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS|= - ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y} + R)dA; = - ∬_(D)(-x \\cdot (2x) - y \\cdot (2y) + 0)dA; = - ∬_(D) - 2x^{2} - 2y^{2}dA; = ∬_(D)2x^{2} + 2y^{2}dA$.\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2x^{2}dA + ∬_(D)2x^{2} + 2y^{2}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4 x 2 + 2 y 2dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198052 1071 "![](images/92/925b2f032f751e4482632c449fff35f544929f3355655c20d6701e640a7b20a3.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198052 1071 "![](images/92/925b2f032f751e4482632c449fff35f544929f3355655c20d6701e640a7b20a3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198052 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle\n$$\n\nis non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides.\n\n![](images/f5/f5a443b0bb234662c8f4a2bc156b458ac09a8db3ce3cc67933f7465a19db2ef9.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1$. The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n$\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle$.\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1$, we have\n$F \\cdot n_{1}|= ⟨0, 0, z\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = ⟨0, 0, 4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = 4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}$.\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n$∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot n_{1}dS; = ∬_(S_{1})4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dS; = 4∬_(D)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA$.\n\nApplying a change of variables\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4∬_(D)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA &= 4∬_(D)r \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= 4\\int_{0}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{1}r^{2}drdθ \\\\\n&= 4\\int_{0}^{2π}dθ\\int_{0}^{1}r^{2}dr \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 2π \\cdot [\\frac{1}{3}r^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 8π(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1$. Therefore, for every point on $S_2$, we have\n$F(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle$.\nTherefore,\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot ndS|= ∬_(S_{2})0 \\cdot ndS; = ∬_(S_{2})0dS; = 0$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot ndS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3} + 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198052 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} =\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\:  z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/f5/f5a443b0bb234662c8f4a2bc156b458ac09a8db3ce3cc67933f7465a19db2ef9.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\pi (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ Therefore, for every point on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198053 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y$ given that $2x^2 + 3y^2 \\leq 1$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198053 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y$ given that $2x^2 + 3y^2 \\leq 1.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198053 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩ \\\\\n⟨2x, 1⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y)= 2x^2 + 3y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= λ∇g(x, y) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y), g_{y}(x, y)⟩ \\\\\n⟨2x, 1⟩ &= λ⟨4x, 6y⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{2x = 4λx; 1 = 6λy⇒{x(1 - 2λ) = 0; λy = \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y) = 1$ implies\n$2(0)^{2} + 3y^{2} = 1⇒y = ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right)$.\n- Assuming $\\lambda = \\dfrac12$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives\n$\\dfrac{1}{2}y = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y = \\dfrac1{3}$.\nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y) =1$ implies\n$2x^{2} + 3(\\frac{1}{3})^{2} = 1⇒x = ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right), \\qquad \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at\n\n$$\n\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)\n$$\n\nand its maximum value is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac23\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198053 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 2x^2 + 3y^2  = 1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ 1=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  = 1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda = \\dfrac12$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right), \\qquad  \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $\\dfrac23.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198054 1071 "The lamina $D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\le x \\le y  ,\\, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\big\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198054 1071 "The lamina $D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\le x \\le y,\\, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\big\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y$. Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198054 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{y} &= ∬_(D)x \\cdot x^{2}ydA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x^{3}ydA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{y}x^{3}ydxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}y[\\int_{0}^{y}x^{3}dx]dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}y \\cdot [\\frac{x^{4}}{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = y}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}y \\cdot \\frac{y^{4}}{4}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{y^{5}}{4}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{y^{6}}{24}]_{y = 0}^{y = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198054 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y .$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{y}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{y}^{5}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{6}}{24}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198055 1071 "A thin plate ![](images/3c/3cfabe3c702883ca24c3188908c4cefeee43adb30797308828edd98374b5b44f.png) with the mass density function ![](images/ac/ac38ea36432f3e278a4c2deb9203fbd5e486e348976e79157a5c6885a576f656.png) is enclosed between the curve ![](images/65/65b9fa371f35bfa31a21b50bc02f64561e4c838d71ebd99bf2d2d1c1be196dff.png) and the ![](images/1f/1fd42a46221d9624ec3d81db0f5c73cd8428ee2f8d80b5d7e4ff67e7018fb194.png)-axis  for ![](images/11/11d74a99b97245c1998471b33f9b9d0974e251345f825c475db4079c02fa273a.png) Find the moment of the plate about the ![](images/b6/b63bded3ee2ad150f576fd05c209caf32bd34853f4a7132fe4ee85b59b06f61a.png)-axis." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198055 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y) = 2x$ is enclosed between the curve $y=1-x^2$ and the $x$-axis for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$. Find the moment of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198055 1074 "The moment of a lamina ![](images/05/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) with the mass density function ![](images/bb/bb3bf01e45585dbd5fe7732a1befa050d734bc46a466fea7f61f42851cfd3af8.png) about the ![](images/d7/d7d15efdd7573bd5b95e49ef05727ed1b8163a06781c393c1d3f27109fdc4eda.png)-axis is given by\n\n![](images/73/73a6e88adb05252ff114153227c13ae4f4adad9b676524179a20d9ae3d045d43.png)\n\nIn our case, ![](images/33/33d4430552670d83a2c303e8ab286d3391f5611450f4e4f0b6ad6ccb58049b0e.png) is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/067543543031a1d8cb4ceb9a7901ad7db77629243adc6658d4a9eab3e98fe09f.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n![](images/03/03ccfab8b682725e94126f9fe59597d6e2886d3aeff64746681d0bbbed072520.png)\n\nand ![](images/17/17c342255c9bffbade441c02123bab3f7154e97b1a6a69a2874935f2dd684806.png) \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n![](images/ca/ca38251cdd0a97967e8e6f4200e94b00571c5cfe2b84fef1991022e4fb976e62.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198055 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/067543543031a1d8cb4ceb9a7901ad7db77629243adc6658d4a9eab3e98fe09f.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\: 0 \\le y \\le 1-x^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = 2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{x} &= ∬_(D)y \\cdot 2xdA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2xydA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1 - x^{2}}2xydydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}2x[\\int_{0}^{1 - x^{2}}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}2x \\cdot [\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 1 - x^{2}}dydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x(1 - x^{2})^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x - 2x^{3} + x^{5}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2}x^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} + \\frac{1}{6}x^{6}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198056 1071 "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda (x) = (x+4)\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$. Find the moment of the rod about the origin.\n\n![](images/7f/7f88ca365b75320795e9d38170a8586399d759696638a4bd78c73ca6c6f44b1a.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198056 1071 "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda (x) = (x+4)\\,\\textrm{kg/m}.$ Find the moment of the rod about the origin.\n\n![](images/7f/7f88ca365b75320795e9d38170a8586399d759696638a4bd78c73ca6c6f44b1a.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198056 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x+4, \\qquad a=0, \\qquad b=3\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{O} &= \\int_{0}^{3}x \\cdot (x + 4)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{3}x^{2} + 4xdx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + 2x^{2}]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3^{3}}{3} + 2 \\cdot 3^{2}) - 0 \\\\\n&= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required moment is $27\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot \\textrm{m}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198056 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by \n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x+4, \\qquad \na=0, \\qquad \nb=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\cdot (x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}+4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+2\\cdot {3}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $27\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot \\textrm{m}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198057 1071 "![](images/46/461c4cb760541ea435620803689e