x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\int_a^b \\, x\\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x) = x^3, \\qquad \na=0, \\qquad \nb=2.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x}= \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{32}{5}\\right)}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that this result makes intuitive sense. Since $\\lambda$ is an increasing function of $x,$ we would expect the center of mass to be located closer to the right endpoint and further from the left endpoint." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198058 1071 "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda(x) =2x\\, \\textrm{kg/m}$, where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?\n\n![](images/8c/8c8386ff979b46cd994f1ace60f681a6590856e6960e7d74c71a29bb3f0f2d1a.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198058 1071 "The thin rod below has the mass density function $\\lambda(x) =2x\\, \\textrm{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?\n\n![](images/8c/8c8386ff979b46cd994f1ace60f681a6590856e6960e7d74c71a29bb3f0f2d1a.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198058 1074 "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b,$ if $\\lambda(x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm = \\int_a^b \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x) = 2x, \\qquad \na=1, \\qquad \nb=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\textrm{kg}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198058 1074 "Given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nif $\\lambda(x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\int_a^b \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x) = 2x, \\qquad a=1, \\qquad b=3\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\int_{1}^{3}2xdx \\\\\n&= [x^{2}]_{1}^{3} \\\\\n&= 3^{2} - 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\textrm{kg}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198059 1071 "For the transformation $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\ncalculate $\\boldsymbol f'$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198059 1071 "For the transformation $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n![](images/d8/d8be5d5bd5fa2cd8b618087109678a4f891b08f3788d4946d07bc1b5e4b39a3a.png)\n\ncalculate $\\boldsymbol f'$ at ![](images/a2/a2dbcf34ea50e451efc96ad2f9a7422a7ae507ca8f74088569110abb25293954.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198059 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x}$, denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x})$, is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y) = 2xy, \\mid \\frac{∂f}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}y) = x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2xy & x^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand $\\boldsymbol{f}'$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(-1) & 1^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198059 1074 "Suppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/17/17588b058b1e1fe934e06679836234f0ef35fe4484a558fcc3ec3b2aafbd2620.png) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/61/613ba027e9564812a8e050efd69fd98fff3f614f7d2ec69eeb787c628ccabd12.png)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/87/87579b882ca6d8c92d4a7adfed575873fd07c36ab20251722c2cc8fc0f0328e8.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/9f/9fc2431aa874c68b23b6640da987b2f3bc37e2261936f7e1656b04bc3fc0030d.png)\n\nand $\\boldsymbol{f}'$ at ![](images/b8/b83c8f9cb4130103d63821f0d0de50ec0628138dcc8faaf16e8cf9fc27f1a994.png) is\n\n![](images/08/0851481c21c553b6e0e06a795815b1845e96e0a14f805e440529d0f4d6f85323.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198060 1071 "For the transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^3$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatr