}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198085 1071 "![](images/ee/eec7d2b67f0b76bb8ec6874cf63f8cb09005f451d684924118983ea1aa39269c.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation\n$2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0$.\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198085 1071 "![](images/ee/eec7d2b67f0b76bb8ec6874cf63f8cb09005f451d684924118983ea1aa39269c.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198085 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS(x, y) &= (x - 0)^{2} + (y - 0)^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + y^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + (\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}))^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2} + \\ln^{2} (\\frac{1}{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(x) = 2x + 2\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}) \\cdot (1)/((\\frac{1}{x})) \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - \\frac{2}{x}\\ln (\\frac{1}{x}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x - \\frac{2}{x}(\\ln 1 - \\ln x) &= 0 \\\\\n2x + 2 ln ⁡ x x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198085 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}))}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x})}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198086 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right).$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198086 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198086 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between\n\n$$\n\\left(0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nand a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} S(x) &= x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + \\left(x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 + x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4} \\\\[5pt] &= x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(