9-0.2981 \\\\  & =0.4038.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198189 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z \\gt -0.76)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8051 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8078 \\,$ |" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198189 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z \\gt -0.76).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8051 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8078 \\,$ |" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198189 1074 "![](images/f8/f88bf9a66694858e9eaf16f616555eda6d9da7de9f62b678bc1f03b32bf9a620.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt -a) = P(Z \\lt a) = \\Phi(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(Z > -0.76)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fbacbbe3f53c73316fd070d95e4c077cd695bdcf884d30a95f6b0850df328c80.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\lt 0.76)$.\n\n![](images/c0/c0f612336c02eee97d6c66b56f0854286b3a23b90053810e9e4fb75b698b5a60.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > -0.76) = P(Z < 0.76) = \\Phi(0.76) = 0.7764\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198189 1074 "![](images/f8/f88bf9a66694858e9eaf16f616555eda6d9da7de9f62b678bc1f03b32bf9a620.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt -a) = P(Z \\lt a) = \\Phi(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P( Z > -0.76).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fbacbbe3f53c73316fd070d95e4c077cd695bdcf884d30a95f6b0850df328c80.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\lt 0.76).$\n\n![](images/c0/c0f612336c02eee97d6c66b56f0854286b3a23b90053810e9e4fb75b698b5a60.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > -0.76)\n= P(Z < 0.76)\n= \\Phi(0.76)\n= 0.7764.\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198190 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9066 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9082 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9099 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9236 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9251 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9370 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9382 \\,$ |" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198190 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9066 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9082 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9099 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9236 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9251 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9370 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9382 \\,$ |" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198190 1074 "![](images/4d/4d6524ff1e32b2c2b4aaf7481dbf63a96f847cd91ce66aeeb58690f37bbe50e5.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt a) = P(Z \\lt -a) = \\Phi(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52d4c1add91819ff1f9dddf609b5753506c26e6cd6ee7f3cb14150ccb9b60971.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\gt 1.54)$.\n\n![](images/7f/7fef056bb5564986a009198685a2951de87ccb8e09c932c6902bfafdcdf0656f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z < - 1.54) &= P(Z > 1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < 1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.9382 \\\\\n&= 0.0618\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198190 1074 "![](images/4d/4d6524ff1e32b2c2b4aaf7481dbf63a96f847cd91ce66aeeb58690f37bbe50e5.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt a) = P(Z \\lt -a) = \\Phi(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P( Z \\lt -1.54).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52d4c1add91819ff1f9dddf609b5753506c26e6cd6ee7f3cb14150ccb9b60971.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\gt 1.54).$\n\n![](images/7f/7fef056bb5564986a009198685a2951de87ccb8e09c932c6902bfafdcdf0656f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-1.54) & =P(Z>1.54) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.54) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.54) \\\\  & =1-0.9382 \\\\  & =0.0618.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198211 1071 "Sketch a representation of the set $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane given that $I = [-1,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, \\, i^2].$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198211 1071 "Sketch a representation of the set $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane given that $I = [-1,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = \\{i \\} \\times [0, \\, i^2]$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198211 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{-0.5} = \\{ -0.5 \\} \\times [0, 0.25] = \\big\\{ (-0.5,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, 0.25] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5, 0)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.25)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc75eac899efcab1d0b6fde12609a3ea8f61d6fec74c739ef3393fad32645a9.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ i \\} \\times [0, i^2] = \\big\\{ (i,k) \\: : \\: k \\in [0, i^2] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i, i^2).$ \n\n![](images/60/60c98be5d97748e9285bb5e9c30bba9781a5ef99245789718a7828b18a6df8fb.png)\n\n \n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i{:}$\n\n![](images/b3/b3a61d686c78c76a4f461c1c771437d31030339b634661da8b8dc4f492db55eb.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198211 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x \\:: \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5$, we have\n\n$$\nX_{-0.5} = \\{-0.5 \\} \\times [0, 0.25] = \\big\\{(-0.5,k) \\:: \\: k \\in [0, 0.25] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5, 0)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.25)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc75eac899efcab1d0b6fde12609a3ea8f61d6fec74c739ef3393fad32645a9.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{i \\} \\times [0, i^2] = \\big\\{(i,k) \\:: \\: k \\in [0, i^2] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i, i^2)$.\n\n![](images/60/60c98be5d97748e9285bb5e9c30bba9781a5ef99245789718a7828b18a6df8fb.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I$, these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i{:}$\n\n![](images/b3/b3a61d686c78c76a4f461c1c771437d31030339b634661da8b8dc4f492db55eb.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198212 1071 "Let $I = [0,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1]$. Sketch a representation of $\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186198212 1071 "Let $I = [0,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1].$ Sketch a representation of $\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane." 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198212 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x \\: : \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for all}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\nX_{0.5} = [-0.5,0] \\times [-1, 1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-0.5, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-0.5, -1),$ $(-0.5, 1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8ad650cb3f2fa4da920a442e8cc1b24af5027595cba4e43138eff2126cf4c102.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1] = \\big\\{ (a,b) \\: : \\: a \\in [-i, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1),$ $(-i,1),$ $(0, 1),$ and $(0, -1).$\n\n![](images/a3/a37cfa508cf3a60536084fbc1e57c5aeac3414904d2ad72ef1deda85dc4eddd8.png)\n\n \n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]{:}$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9a611629f4370c57e77a057439c759670afa17c14c4b2339e1d471f616ea72.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186198212 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x \\:: \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for all}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5$, we have\n\n$$\nX_{0.5} = [-0.5,0] \\times [-1, 1] = \\big\\{(a,b) \\:: \\: a \\in [-0.5, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-0.5, -1)$, $(-0.5, 1)$, $(0, 1)$, and $(0, -1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8ad650cb3f2fa4da920a442e8cc1b24af5027595cba4e43138eff2126cf4c102.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1] = \\big\\{(a,b) \\:: \\: a \\in [-i, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1)$, $(-i,1)$, $(0, 1)$, and $(0, -1)$.\n\n![](images/a3/a37cfa508cf3a60536084fbc1e57c5aeac3414904d2ad72ef1deda85dc4eddd8.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]{:}$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9a611629f4370c57e77a057439c759670afa17c14c4b2339e1d471f616ea72.png)" 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186203725 1071 "Find $(-2\\mathbf{a}+5\\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c}$, if $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = -3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$." 13194139533385 17592186045585 false] [17592186203725 1071 "Find $(-2\\mathbf{a}+5\\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c},$ if $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = -3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186203725 1074 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2a+5b)\\times c & =(-2a)\\times c+(5b)\\times c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2a)\\times c+(5b)\\times c & =-2(a\\times c)+5(b\\times c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(a\\times c)+5(b\\times c) & =-2\\cdot (i)+5\\cdot (-3i+j) \\\\  & =-2i-15i+5j \\\\  & =-17i+5j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533385 17592186045585 true] [17592186203725 1074 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n$(a ± b) \\cdot c|= a \\cdot c ± b \\cdot c; c \\cdot (a ± b)|= c \\cdot a ± c \\cdot b$\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n$(λa) \\cdot b|= λ(a \\cdot b); a \\cdot (λb)|= λ(a \\cdot b)$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-2a + 5b) \\cdot c &= (-2a) \\cdot c + (5b) \\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n$(-2a) \\cdot c + (5b) \\cdot c|=-2(a \\cdot c) + 5(b \\cdot c)$\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(a \\cdot c) + 5(b \\cdot c) &= -2 \\cdot (i) + 5 \\cdot (-3i + j) \\\\\n&=-2i - 15i + 5j \\\\\n&=-17i + 5j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139